          "text": "Now I understand the current tool. It's very basic:\n- Takes `<ip:port>`\n- Uses `/dev/tcp` trick with TCP only\n- Reports OPEN/CLOSED\n\nThe user wants:\n1. **Smarter and dynamic**: check all types (TCP, UDP, maybe ICMP?)\n2. **Report inline if port open or not** — inline reporting\n3. **Metadata about the port** — service name from /etc/services, description, common service info\n\nLet me think about the design:\n\n**Input formats to support:**\n- `pos network checkport <ip:port>` (current)\n- `pos network checkport <ip>:<port>` with proto flag: `--tcp` / `--udp`, or `tcp/80`, `udp/53`\n- Multiple ports: `pos network checkport 192.168.1.1 80 443` or comma-separated `192.168.1.1:80,443,8080`\n- Maybe CIDR-ish host ranges? Keep it focused.\n\n**Checking methods:**\n- TCP: `/dev/tcp` (bash builtin, no deps) — current approach. Can also do a banner grab for metadata? The user said \"metadata about port\" — likely service name. Banner grab could be a nice bonus but flaky. Keep it: service lookup from /etc/services + well-known metadata table.\n- UDP: `/dev/udp` — the bash trick works but UDP is connectionless: `cat < /dev/null > /dev/udp/host/port` \"succeeds\" immediately regardless because there's no handshake. UDP open/closed is fundamentally unreliable (firewalls drop). Better approach: \n  - Send a probe (empty packet) and wait for ICMP port-unreachable → closed. Bash /dev/udp can't detect that easily.\n  - `nc -u -z -w` does this: nc -u -z sends a zero-length UDP packet and waits for ICMP unreachable. If it times out → \"open|filtered\". If ICMP unreachable → closed.\n  - So for UDP, use `nc` if available (`nc -u -z -w1 host port`), else fall back to reporting \"unknown (no nc)\".\n  - Another technique: use `nc -u -w2` and treat timeout as \"open|filtered\" (common for DNS, NTP).\n  \n- ICMP: ping the host — metadata about host reachability, not a port. Could add a `--ping` or auto-ping before port check to report host up/down. That's a nice \"smarter\" touch: if host doesn't respond to ping, still try TCP (some hosts block ICMP). Report host status inline.\n\n**Metadata about port:**\n- Service name from /etc/services: `grep -w \"$port/tcp\" /etc/services` and `$port/udp`.\n- A built-in table of common ports (22 ssh, 80 http, 443 https, 53 dns, 8080 http-alt, etc.) with a short description — richer than /etc/services for famous ports. Could merge: built-in table first, fall back to /etc/services.\n- Optionally banner grab (TCP): connect and read the first line within 1-2s → service banner (e.g., SSH banner \"SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6\"). That's REAL metadata. `exec 3<>/dev/tcp/...` then `head -1 <&3`. For known plaintext protocols (ssh, smtp, ftp, http), banners are very informative. For TLS ports, could grab TLS certificate? That's a lot. Maybe just banner grab opportunistically. Keep it simple: banner grab with short timeout, only for TCP.\n\n**Design:**\n\n```\nUsage: pos network checkport <target> [more targets] [options]\n\nTargets:\n  <ip>:<port>             TCP (default)\n  <ip>:<port>/udp         UDP\n  <ip>:<port>/tcp         explicit TCP\n  tcp://<ip>:<port>       alternative\n  udp://<ip>:<port>\n  <ip> <port> [port...]   host + one or more ports (all TCP, or all UDP with --udp)\n\nOptions:\n  --udp / -u              check UDP instead of TCP (with host + ports form)\n  --tcp / -t              force TCP (default)\n  --no-banner             skip banner grab\n  --timeout <sec>         per-check timeout (default 2)\n```\n\nInline report per port:\n```\n  192.168.1.1:22 (ssh)        OPEN     banner: SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6p1\n  192.168.1.1:80 (http)       OPEN     banner: HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\n  192.168.1.1:443 (https)     OPEN     (no banner)\n  192.168.1.1:53/udp (dns)    open|filtered   (UDP probe sent, no reply)\n  192.168.1.1:8080 (http-alt) CLOSED\n```\n\nFor UDP: state can be:\n- CLOSED (ICMP port unreachable received → nc returns failure with -z)\n- open|filtered (no reply within timeout — could be open or filtered)\n\nWith nc -u -z: exit 0 = open, exit 1 = closed (unreachable), timeout = open|filtered. nc -z with -u and -w: nc returns 0 if it receives anything; ICMP unreachable → non-zero... Actually behavior varies by nc implementation. OpenBSD nc: `nc -u -z -w2 host port` → 0 if port open-ish? For UDP, nc -z exits 0 if it gets ANY response, 1 on ICMP error or timeout. Hmm, with -w2 timeout, if no response at all → exit 1 (timeout). So we can't distinguish timeout (filtered) from closed unless we get ICMP unreachable... nc exit code: 0 = success, 1 = generic error (could be timeout OR ICMP unreachable). We can detect ICMP unreachable via stderr message? Different nc versions differ.\n\nSimpler pragmatic approach for UDP:\n- If nc available: `timeout 3 nc -u -z -w2 host port`; exit 0 → OPEN (got response); exit 1 → CLOSED or FILTERED (report \"closed|filtered (no reply)\"); \n- If nc missing: send via bash /dev/udp? No — bash /dev/udp always succeeds. Alternative without nc: use ping-style? No. For UDP, without nc, we can try `/dev/udp` write + read with timeout — read would time out → \"no reply (open|filtered)\". Actually bash can do: `exec 3<>/dev/udp/host/port; printf '' >&3; timeout 1 head -c0 <&3` — hmm reading 0 bytes... This is fragile. \n\nPractical: use nc if present; else use bash /dev/udp write and classify \"no reply → open|filtered\". But honestly, most homelab Debian systems have nc (ncat from nmap or openbsd-netcat). Check `command -v nc`.\n\nAlso could check for `nmap` as an option? The user said \"its very basic\" — the tool is basic on purpose (no deps). Adding nc as optional dep with graceful fallback is fine. And keep `--udp` usable without nc via /dev/udp + classification \"no reply (open|filtered)\" — that's honest.\n\nHmm wait — there's actually a decent pure-bash UDP trick: send the packet, then use a second `/dev/udp` read with timeout. If we get ICMP port unreachable... bash doesn't surface that. So no. nc it is.\n\n**Metadata table:** built-in map of common ports with descriptions. Also /etc/services lookup for any port. Precedence: built-in table (curated descriptions) > /etc/services (official names) > \"unknown\".\n\nCommon ports to include (tcp & udp):\n- 20 ftp-data, 21 ftp, 22 ssh, 23 telnet, 25 smtp, 53 dns (udp+tcp), 67/68 dhcp (udp), 69 tftp (udp), 80 http, 88 kerberos, 110 pop3, 111 rpcbind, 123 ntp (udp), 135 msrpc, 137-139 netbios, 143 imap, 161 snmp (udp), 179 bgp, 194 irc, 389 ldap, 443 https, 445 smb, 465 smtps, 514 syslog (udp), 515 lpr, 548 afp, 587 submission, 631 ipp, 636 ldaps, 873 rsync, 993 imaps, 995 pop3s, 1080 socks, 1433 mssql, 1521 oracle, 1701 l2tp (udp), 1723 pptp, 1812/1813 radius (udp), 2049 nfs, 2181 zookeeper, 2375/2376 docker, 3000 grafana/dev, 3128 squid, 3268/3269 ldap gc, 3306 mysql, 3389 rdp, 4369 rabbitmq epmd, 5000 upnp/registry, 5001, 5432 postgres, 5672 amqp, 5900 vnc, 5984 couchdb, 5985 winrm, 6000 X11, 6379 redis, 6443 k8s, 7001, 8000 http-alt, 8006 proxmox, 8080 http-alt, 8081, 8443 https-alt, 8888, 9000 sonarqube/php-fpm, 9001, 9090 prometheus, 9092 kafka, 9100 printer, 9200 elasticsearch, 9418 git, 9997 splunk, 10000, 11211 memcached, 15672 rabbitmq mgmt, 20000, 25565 minecraft, 27017 mongodb, 32400 plex, 5000/5005... \n\nI'll build a curated subset of ~50 common ones and fall back to /etc/services.\n\n**Banner grab (TCP):**\n```bash\ngrab_banner() {\n    local ip=$1 port=$2 tmo=$3 out\n    out=\"$(timeout \"$tmo\" bash -c \"exec 3<>/dev/tcp/$ip/$port; head -c 200 <&3\" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\\r\\n')\"\n    [ -n \"$out\" ] && printf '%s' \"$out\"\n}\n```\nCareful: `head -c 200` will wait until 200 bytes or EOF or timeout — with timeout 1-2s. For SSH it gets the banner + closes. For HTTP, some servers keep the connection open (HTTP/1.1 keep-alive), head -c 200 times out with partial — fine, timeout kills it. Also need to be careful reading can block forever → the timeout command handles it.\n\nAlternatively read the first LINE: `head -n1` — but if the server sends no newline (TLS), it blocks until timeout. `head -c 200` with timeout is fine.\n\nAlso close fd properly. The subshell handles it.\n\n**Host reachability:** optional ping first: `ping -c1 -W1` → report \"host up/down\" — but hosts blocking ICMP shouldn't be flagged as down; just note it. Maybe skip ping by default; add `--ping` flag? Or auto: do a quick ping; if no reply, still do the port checks (host may block ICMP), but note \"no ICMP reply\". Keep inline output tidy: only show ping info when asked or when it fails? Let me make it: if `--ping` given, do ping and report. Otherwise skip. Hmm, the user said \"check all types\" — maybe they mean TCP and UDP (port types), not ICMP. I'll include a `--ping` for host-level check as a bonus, default off to keep output inline-clean.\n\nActually \"check all types\" — likely means TCP + UDP at least. ICMP is not a port. I'll add --ping as optional.\n\n**\"report inline\"** — user wants one-line per target with status + metadata. Good: single compact line per check, e.g.:\n\n```\nOPEN   ✔ 192.168.1.1:22   ssh          SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6p1 Debian-3ubuntu13\nCLOSED ✖ 192.168.1.1:80   http         (no service)\n```\n\n**Return code:** exit non-zero if any checked port is closed (useful for scripts)? Current tool: exits 0 always basically. Let me set exit 1 if any checked port is not OPEN. That's script-friendly. But careful: UDP \"open|filtered\" — count as open? For exit code purposes, treat \"open|filtered\" as open-ish? Hmm — report it, exit code: non-open TCP → 1; UDP filtered → treat as 2 (unknown)? Keep simple: exit 1 if any TCP port closed. UDP filtered → not counted as failure. I'll document.\n\nNow the format details. Let me keep the current `<ip:port>` interface working exactly (backwards compat), and add:\n- `ip:port/tcp|udp`\n- `ip:port:proto`? No — `/tcp` suffix reads natural (like /etc/services).\n- Multiple targets as multiple args.\n- `--udp` flag applies to plain `<ip:port>` args (and with host + ports form).\n- Also support `ip port1 port2` when first arg has no colon? That changes semantics: currently `checkport <ip:port>` only. If first arg is an IP without port and second is a number → host + ports form. Detect: if arg1 has no colon and arg2 is numeric → host form. Nice to have; adds complexity. Let me support it: after parsing options, if first non-flag arg contains ':', it's a target list; else first arg = host, rest = ports.\n\nEdge cases:\n- IPv6: `[::1]:80` — /dev/tcp supports `[::1]:80`? bash /dev/tcp/::1/80 — needs brackets: `/dev/tcp/[::1]/80`? Actually bash: `/dev/tcp/host/port` where host can be `::1`? Bash treats everything between first and last slash... `/dev/tcp/::1/80` — bash splits on slashes: \"::1\" and \"80\" — that works? It resolves via getaddrinfo with AF_UNSPEC? bash uses host=::1 port=80 — should work. But parsing `[::1]:80` with my `%:*` splits — `ip=\"[::1]\"` port=80 — strip brackets when using. Let me handle: strip surrounding brackets from ip. And for `port#*:` on `[::1]:80` → `80` — good since `#*:` takes everything after first colon → `::1]:80`... wait: `port=\"${target#*:}\"` on `[::1]:80` gives `]:80` — wrong! `#*:` strips up to FIRST colon → `]:80`. And `ip=\"${target%:*}\"` gives `[::1` — wrong. So IPv6 needs care: use last colon for port: `port=\"${target##*:}\"`, `ip=\"${target%:*}\"` then strip `[`/`]`. For IPv4 same result. But host form `<host> <port>` — port is a separate arg, no parsing issue. And `ip:port/udp` — strip /udp suffix first. Order: strip proto suffix from the port part.\n\nCareful with `target##*:` when target is `host:port/udp`: `##*:` → `port/udp`, then strip `/udp`. And with IPv6 `[::1]:80` → `80`. Good. `ip=\"${target%:*}\"` on `[::1]:80` → `[::1]` → strip brackets → `::1`. On IPv4 `1.2.3.4:80` → `1.2.3.4`. Good. Edge: `host:port` where host is hostname — fine.\n\nWhat about `target##*:` when the host part has no colon but the whole is `host:80` → `80`. Good.\n\n**Banner metadata** might reveal service name from banner too (e.g., SSH-2.0 → ssh). If /etc/services says \"unknown\" but banner starts with \"SSH-2.0\", we could show \"ssh?\". Cute but adds complexity — I'll show banner as-is; the metadata name from table.\n\n**UDP specific metadata:** some ports are UDP-only (dhcp 67/68, ntp 123, snmp 161, tftp 69, dns 53 both). The table lookup should be proto-aware: look up `port/tcp` or `port/udp` in /etc/services. My curated table: map key \"80/tcp\" → name, \"53/udp\" → name, with \"53/tcp\" also → dns. Simplify: store per-port default name; if udp and /etc/services has an entry, prefer it.\n\nLet me now also check: does the repo use any helpers for pings etc.? Look at lib/common.sh for run/spawn, err, log helpers — the tool currently doesn't source common.sh. The convention says tools should source lib/common.sh. The current checkport doesn't. Should I keep it standalone? Convention: \"Tools must run standalone from /usr/local/bin after install (source lib/common.sh via fallback chain)\". Most pos tools source it. But this one doesn't — it's standalone. Adding common.sh dependency is fine (it's installed to /usr/local/bin too). But common.sh might require config dirs etc. Let me check what common.sh provides (err/log/warn/run/spawn helpers). To keep the change low-risk and self-contained, I could keep it standalone. But consistency... Let me peek at lib/common.sh quickly.\n\nAlso check whether there's a POS_FLAGS header convention for completion — other tools have `# POS_FLAGS:`. Adding `# POS_FLAGS: --udp --tcp --no-banner --timeout --ping` would feed tab-completion. The repo's other tools do this (matrix-sender doesn't have POS_FLAGS — only POS_SUBCMDS). checkport has no subcommands, so POS_SUBCMDS not needed. POS_FLAGS would help completion. Let me add `# POS_FLAGS: --tcp --udp --ping --no-banner --timeout`.\n\nLet me check how POS_FLAGS is parsed (completions/pos.bash gen) — it feeds flags for completion. Adding it is safe; make gen regenerates.\n\nNow write the new tool. Let me design output:\n\n```\n$ pos network checkport 192.168.1.1 22 80 443 --udp 53\nChecking 192.168.1.1 ...\n\n  22/tcp   ssh    OPEN     SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6p1 Debian-3ubuntu13.4\n  80/tcp   http   CLOSED\n 443/tcp   https  OPEN     (no banner)\n  53/udp   dns    open|filtered\n```\n\nHmm — \"report inline if port open or not and metadata about port\" — one line per port: status + service name + banner. \n\nFormat per line: `STATE ip:port/proto service banner`. Let me align columns for readability with printf.\n\nActually simpler and greppable: `ip:port/proto  state  service  [banner]`.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n# POS: network checkport — Check TCP/UDP port connectivity with service info + banner\n# POS_FLAGS: --tcp --udp --ping --no-banner --timeout\n\nusage() {\n    cat <<EOF\nUsage: pos network checkport <target> [target...] [options]\n\nCheck if ports are reachable — TCP (default) and UDP — and report the\nservice name and (TCP) banner inline.\n\nTargets:\n  <ip|host>:<port>            TCP check (default)\n  <ip|host>:<port>/udp        UDP check (needs 'nc'; pure-bash fallback reports no reply)\n  <ip|host> <port> [port...]  host form: all ports checked as TCP (or UDP with --udp)\n  IPv6: [::1]:80\n\nOptions:\n  -t, --tcp          force TCP (default)\n  -u, --udp          check UDP instead of TCP (host form / plain ip:port)\n  --ping             also ping the host first (reports host up/down)\n  --no-banner        skip TCP banner grab (faster)\n      --timeout <s>  per-check timeout in seconds (default 2)\n\nExit status: 0 if all TCP checks were open (UDP no-reply and ping do not fail);\n             1 if any checked TCP port is closed or a target is invalid.\n\nExamples:\n  pos network checkport 192.168.1.1:22\n  pos network checkport 10.0.0.5 22 80 443\n  pos network checkport 10.0.0.5:53/udp 10.0.0.5:123/udp\n  pos network checkport [fd00::1]:443\nEOF\n    exit 0\n}\n```\n\nService metadata: a curated associative-ish lookup via case statement (port/proto) → name:desc. Let me build function `port_meta()`:\n\n```bash\nport_meta() {\n    local port=\"$1\" proto=\"$2\" name=\"\" desc=\"\"\n    case \"$port/$proto\" in\n        20/tcp) name=\"ftp-data\"; desc=\"FTP data\" ;;\n        21/tcp) name=\"ftp\"; desc=\"FTP\" ;;\n        22/tcp) name=\"ssh\"; desc=\"SSH\" ;;\n        23/tcp) name=\"telnet\"; desc=\"Telnet\" ;;\n        25/tcp) name=\"smtp\"; desc=\"SMTP\" ;;\n        53/tcp|53/udp) name=\"dns\"; desc=\"DNS\" ;;\n        67/udp|68/udp) name=\"dhcp\"; desc=\"DHCP\" ;;\n        69/udp) name=\"tftp\"; desc=\"TFTP\" ;;\n        80/tcp) name=\"http\"; desc=\"HTTP\" ;;\n        88/tcp|88/udp) name=\"kerberos\"; desc=\"Kerberos\" ;;\n        110/tcp) name=\"pop3\"; desc=\"POP3\" ;;\n        111/tcp|111/udp) name=\"rpcbind\"; desc=\"RPC portmapper\" ;;\n        123/udp) name=\"ntp\"; desc=\"NTP\" ;;\n        135/tcp) name=\"msrpc\"; desc=\"MS-RPC\" ;;\n        137/udp) name=\"netbios-ns\"; desc=\"NetBIOS name\" ;;\n        139/tcp) name=\"netbios-ssn\"; desc=\"NetBIOS session\" ;;\n        143/tcp) name=\"imap\"; desc=\"IMAP\" ;;\n        161/udp) name=\"snmp\"; desc=\"SNMP\" ;;\n        179/tcp) name=\"bgp\"; desc=\"BGP\" ;;\n        194/tcp) name=\"irc\"; desc=\"IRC\" ;;\n        389/tcp) name=\"ldap\"; desc=\"LDAP\" ;;\n        443/tcp) name=\"https\"; desc=\"HTTPS\" ;;\n        445/tcp) name=\"smb\"; desc=\"SMB/CIFS\" ;;\n        465/tcp) name=\"smtps\"; desc=\"SMTPS\" ;;\n        514/udp) name=\"syslog\"; desc=\"Syslog\" ;;\n        548/tcp) name=\"afp\"; desc=\"Apple Filing\" ;;\n        587/tcp) name=\"submission\"; desc=\"SMTP submission\" ;;\n        631/tcp) name=\"ipp\"; desc=\"IPP printing\" ;;\n        636/tcp) name=\"ldaps\"; desc=\"LDAPS\" ;;\n        873/tcp) name=\"rsync\"; desc=\"rsync\" ;;\n        993/tcp) name=\"imaps\"; desc=\"IMAPS\" ;;\n        995/tcp) name=\"pop3s\"; desc=\"POP3S\" ;;\n        1080/tcp) name=\"socks\"; desc=\"SOCKS proxy\" ;;\n        1433/tcp) name=\"mssql\"; desc=\"MS SQL Server\" ;;\n        1521/tcp) name=\"oracle\"; desc=\"Oracle DB\" ;;\n        1701/udp) name=\"l2tp\"; desc=\"L2TP\" ;;\n        1723/tcp) name=\"pptp\"; desc=\"PPTP\" ;;\n        1812/udp|1813/udp) name=\"radius\"; desc=\"RADIUS\" ;;\n        2049/tcp|2049/udp) name=\"nfs\"; desc=\"NFS\" ;;\n        2181/tcp) name=\"zookeeper\"; desc=\"ZooKeeper\" ;;\n        2375/tcp) name=\"docker\"; desc=\"Docker API (unencrypted!)\" ;;\n        2376/tcp) name=\"docker-tls\"; desc=\"Docker API (TLS)\" ;;\n        3000/tcp) name=\"grafana\"; desc=\"Grafana/dev\" ;;\n        3128/tcp) name=\"squid\"; desc=\"Squid proxy\" ;;\n        3268/tcp|3269/tcp) name=\"ldap-gc\"; desc=\"AD Global Catalog\" ;;\n        3306/tcp) name=\"mysql\"; desc=\"MySQL/MariaDB\" ;;\n        3389/tcp) name=\"rdp\"; desc=\"RDP\" ;;\n        4369/tcp) name=\"epmd\"; desc=\"Erlang port mapper\" ;;\n        5000/tcp) name=\"upnp\"; desc=\"UPnP\" ;;\n        5432/tcp) name=\"postgres\"; desc=\"PostgreSQL\" ;;\n        5672/tcp) name=\"amqp\"; desc=\"AMQP\" ;;\n        5900/tcp) name=\"vnc\"; desc=\"VNC\" ;;\n        5985/tcp) name=\"winrm\"; desc=\"WinRM\" ;;\n        6000/tcp) name=\"x11\"; desc=\"X11\" ;;\n        6379/tcp) name=\"redis\"; desc=\"Redis\" ;;\n        6443/tcp) name=\"k8s-api\"; desc=\"Kubernetes API\" ;;\n        8006/tcp) name=\"proxmox\"; desc=\"Proxmox\" ;;\n        8080/tcp) name=\"http-alt\"; desc=\"HTTP alternate\" ;;\n        8443/tcp) name=\"https-alt\"; desc=\"HTTPS alternate\" ;;\n        8888/tcp) name=\"http-alt2\"; desc=\"HTTP alternate\" ;;\n        9090/tcp) name=\"prometheus\"; desc=\"Prometheus\" ;;\n        9092/tcp) name=\"kafka\"; desc=\"Kafka\" ;;\n        9100/tcp) name=\"printer\"; desc=\"network printer\" ;;\n        9200/tcp) name=\"elasticsearch\"; desc=\"Elasticsearch\" ;;\n        9418/tcp) name=\"git\"; desc=\"git daemon\" ;;\n        9997/tcp) name=\"splunk\"; desc=\"Splunk\" ;;\n        11211/tcp