     Export read-only\n  --guest             Allow guest access (ANY user on the network — warns)\n  --users <u1,u2>     Restrict to these Samba users (comma-separated)\n\nExamples:\n  pos share smb-server share /mnt/hdd media --users bob,alice\n  pos share smb-server share /mnt/hdd/media --read-only\n  pos share smb-server adduser bob\n  pos share smb-server list\n  pos share smb-server unshare media\nEOF\n    exit 0\n}\n\ncmd=\"${1:-}\"\ncase \"$cmd\" in\n    -h|--help) usage ;;\n    status|share|unshare|list|adduser|deluser|reload|enable|disable) ;;\n    \"\") err \"Missing command (status|share|unshare|list|adduser|deluser|reload|enable|disable)\" ;;\n    *) err \"Unknown command '$cmd' (see --help)\" ;;\nesac\nshift || true   # hmm — careful. After validating cmd, shift so $1 is next arg.\n\nSMB_CONF=/etc/samba/smb.conf\n```\n\nWait, careful with `shift`: the original NFS tool doesn't shift; it uses `${2:-}`. For the smb server, `share` has optional flags. Let me parse flags within the share case. Positional: `pos share smb-server share /mnt/hdd media --read-only --users bob,alice`.\n\nSimplest: inside share, loop over args. Since the tool case dispatches on $1 (cmd), args start at $2. Let me collect:\n\n```bash\n    share)\n        path=\"${2:-}\"\n        [ -n \"$path\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-server share <path> [name] [opts]\"\n        case \"$path\" in -*) err \"Usage: ...\" ;; esac\n        shift 2\n        name=\"\" ro=0 guest=0 users=\"\"\n        while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n            case \"$1\" in\n                --read-only) ro=1; shift ;;\n                --guest) guest=1; shift ;;\n                --users) [ $# -ge 2 ] || err \"--users needs a value\"; users=\"$2\"; shift 2 ;;\n                -*) err \"unknown option '$1'\" ;;\n                *) name=\"$1\"; shift ;;\n            esac\n        done\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm but the main case dispatches on cmd from $1. Using `shift 2` inside a case arm modifies the positional params — fine.\n\nActually, to keep style consistent with the NFS tool (which uses ${2:-} etc.), I could use a small helper. But flags are needed for read-only/guest/users. I'll do the while-loop approach in the share arm.\n\nFor require_root_dir validation — path must be absolute + exist. Reuse pattern from nfs-server.\n\nShare block generation:\n\n```bash\nmarker_start=\"# >>> pos-managed share: $name\"\nmarker_end=\"# <<< end pos-managed share\"\n\ntmp=\"$(mktemp)\"\nawk -v s=\"$marker_start\" -v e=\"$marker_end\" '\n    $0 == s {inblock=1}\n    $0 == e {inblock=0; next}\n    !inblock {print}\n' \"$SMB_CONF\" > \"$tmp\"\ncat >> \"$tmp\" <<EOF\n\n$marker_start\n[$name]\n   path = $path\n   browseable = yes\n   read only = no\n   guest ok = no\nEOF\nif [ \"$ro\" -eq 1 ]; then sed -i 's/^   read only = no/   read only = yes/' ...\n```\n\nBetter: build the block with a variable. Let me construct:\n\n```bash\nopts=\"\"\n[ \"$ro\" -eq 1 ] && opts=\"$opts read only = yes\" || opts=\"$opts read only = no\"\n...\n```\n\nCleaner:\n\n```bash\nblock=\"\n$marker_start\n[$name]\n   path = $path\n   browseable = yes\n   read only = $([ \"$ro\" -eq 1 ] && echo yes || echo no)\n   guest ok = $([ \"$guest\" -eq 1 ] && echo yes || echo no)\"\n[ -n \"$users\" ] && block=\"$block\n   valid users = $users\"\nblock=\"$block\n$marker_end\"\n```\n\nHmm, the `$([ ... ] && echo yes || echo no)` is a bit clever — but readable enough? Let me write it more explicitly:\n\n```bash\nro_val=no; [ \"$ro\" -eq 1 ] && ro_val=yes\nguest_val=no; [ \"$guest\" -eq 1 ] && guest_val=yes\nblock=\"[$name]\n   path = $path\n   browseable = yes\n   read only = $ro_val\n   guest ok = $guest_val\"\n[ -n \"$users\" ] && block=\"$block\n   valid users = $users\"\n```\n\nWarnings:\n- If guest: warn \"guest ok = yes — ANY network user can access $path\"\n- If no valid users AND not guest: warn \"No valid users — any Samba account can access\" (like NFS generic warn).\n\nThen:\n```bash\n{\n    echo\n    echo \"$marker_start\"\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$block\"\n    echo \"$marker_end\"\n} >> \"$tmp\"\nsudo cp \"$tmp\" \"$SMB_CONF\"\nrm -f \"$tmp\"\nsmb_reload\nlog \"Share added: [$name] → $path\"\nnotify_send \"SMB share added: $name ($path)\"\n```\n\nWhere smb_reload() validates and reloads:\n```bash\nsmb_reload() {\n    sudo testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" >/dev/null\n    sudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config\n}\n```\n\ntestparm -s returns nonzero if config is invalid — with set -euo pipefail, if testparm fails the script dies (after sudo cp already happened...). Hmm — order matters: validate BEFORE cp. testparm on the temp file first:\n\n```bash\nvalidate_conf() {\n    sudo testparm -s \"$1\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || err \"Invalid smb.conf — not applied (see testparm -s /etc/samba/smb.conf)\"\n}\n```\n\nActually testparm doesn't need root to validate a file: `testparm -s /etc/samba/smb.conf` — but if the file is only root-readable? /etc/samba/smb.conf is typically 644. Use plain testparm -s on the tmp file before cp:\n\n```bash\ntestparm -s \"$tmp\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { rm -f \"$tmp\"; err \"Invalid smb.conf — changes not applied\"; }\nsudo cp \"$tmp\" \"$SMB_CONF\"\nrm -f \"$tmp\"\nsudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config\n```\n\nAnd for `reload` command: `sudo testparm -s && sudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config` — testparm -s with sudo not needed. But the reload should validate the actual file: `testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" >/dev/null` then reload.\n\nNote: sudo testparm vs testparm — testparm reads the file; as non-root can read 644. Use plain testparm for validation. But `smbcontrol smbd reload-config` needs root (smbd runs as root; the smbcontrol uses a pipe/socket — needs root). Use sudo.\n\nHmm, smbcontrol: `sudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config`. Good.\n\nunshare:\n```bash\nunshare)\n    name=\"${2:-}\"\n    [ -n \"$name\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-server unshare <name>\"\n    tmp=\"$(mktemp)\"\n    awk -v s=\"# >>> pos-managed share: $name\" -v e=\"# <<< end pos-managed share\" '\n        $0 == s {inblock=1}\n        $0 == e {inblock=0; next}\n        !inblock {print}\n    ' \"$SMB_CONF\" > \"$tmp\"\n    if cmp -s \"$tmp\" \"$SMB_CONF\"; then\n        rm -f \"$tmp\"\n        warn \"No share '$name' found in $SMB_CONF\"\n        exit 0\n    fi\n    sudo cp \"$tmp\" \"$SMB_CONF\"\n    rm -f \"$tmp\"\n    sudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config\n    log \"Removed share: $name\"\n    notify_send \"SMB share removed: $name\"\n    ;;\n```\n\nstatus:\n```bash\nstatus)\n    if systemctl is-active --quiet smbd 2>/dev/null; then\n        ok \"smbd: running\"\n    else\n        warn \"smbd: not running (enable with 'pos share smb-server enable')\"\n    fi\n    echo\n    section \"Shares\"\n    list_shares\n    echo\n    section \"Users\"\n    pdbedit -L 2>/dev/null | awk '{print \"  \"$1}' || echo \" (none)\"\n    ;;\n```\n\nHmm, pdbedit -L outputs lines like \"bob:1000:\" — awk '{print $1}' would print \"bob:1000:\". Better: `pdbedit -L 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f1 | sed 's/^/  /'`. pdbedit needs root? `pdbedit -L` as non-root: it reads /var/lib/samba/private/passdb.tdb — may need root. Use sudo pdbedit -L. Let me use `sudo pdbedit -L`.\n\nlist_shares: testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" 2>/dev/null | grep '^\\[' | grep -v '\\[global\\]' ... but testparm -s output includes [global]. Also, if smb.conf doesn't exist (package not configured), testparm fails. Fallback to \"(none)\". And parse shares:\n\n```bash\nlist_shares() {\n    local out\n    out=\"$(testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" 2>/dev/null | grep '^\\[' || true)\"\n    if [ -z \"$out\" ]; then\n        echo \" (none)\"\n        return\n    fi\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$out\" | grep -v '^\\[global\\]$' | sed 's/\\[\\(.*\\)\\]/  \\1/'\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — testparm -s prints the parsed smb.conf; sections appear as [global], [share]. There may be default sections from the package ([printers], [homes])? The Debian default smb.conf has [global] and example [printers]/[homes] commented? Actually default Debian smb.conf includes [global], [homes], [printers] sections (not commented, but with printable=no etc.?). I'll just list what testparm reports, minus [global]. Fine.\n\nAlso if $SMB_CONF missing → testparm -s errors → out empty → \"(none)\". Good.\n\nlist: just list_shares.\n\nadduser:\n```bash\nadduser)\n    user=\"${2:-}\"\n    [ -n \"$user\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-server adduser <user>\"\n    id -u \"$user\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || err \"No system user '$user' — create it first (e.g. sudo adduser $user)\"\n    sudo smbpasswd -a \"$user\"\n    log \"Samba user added: $user\"\n    notify_send \"SMB user added: $user\"\n    ;;\n```\n\ndeluser:\n```bash\ndeluser)\n    user=\"${2:-}\"\n    [ -n \"$user\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-server deluser <user>\"\n    sudo smbpasswd -x \"$user\"\n    log \"Samba user removed: $user\"\n    notify_send \"SMB user removed: $user\"\n    ;;\n```\n\nenable/disable like NFS with smbd.\n\nAlso — the package default: /etc/samba/smb.conf — Debian installs one. But on a fresh install it exists. If missing, share would fail on awk reading missing file. Add a guard in share: `[ -f \"$SMB_CONF\" ] || err \"No smb.conf at $SMB_CONF — is samba installed?\"`. Actually testparm validation covers it (testparm -s missing file → error). But awk on missing file errors too — with set -e... awk with a missing file: `awk '...' /nonexistent` → error → set -e kills with traceback. Guard at top after deps:\n\n```bash\n[ -f \"$SMB_CONF\" ] || err \"No smb.conf at $SMB_CONF (is samba installed?)\"\n```\n\nHmm but status/list should still work if config missing. Guard only in share/unshare. Actually the tool's deps check (smbd etc.) ensures samba installed; samba package installs smb.conf. Put the guard inside share and unshare.\n\nWait — one more thing: `smbcontrol smbd reload-config` when smbd not running → error. In share/unshare/reload, if server isn't running, reload-config fails. Acceptable? The NFS tool's share runs exportfs -ra which also needs nfs-server running (or works standalone). For robustness: reload = validate + try reload-config with `|| warn`. Let me do:\n\n```bash\nreload_config() {\n    testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo \"smb.conf invalid:\" >&2; testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" 2>&1 | grep -i error >&2 || true; exit 1; }\n    if systemctl is-active --quiet smbd 2>/dev/null; then\n        sudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config\n        log \"smbd config reloaded\"\n    else\n        warn \"smbd not running — config validated but not loaded (enable first)\"\n    fi\n}\n```\n\nCareful with set -e: `testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { ...; exit 1; }` — fine. But inside function called in subshell? It's called directly, exit 1 exits script. OK.\n\nHmm, `testparm` prints errors to stderr; the `|| { echo ...; grep ... || true; exit 1; }` — the grep may find nothing → `|| true` guards set -e... wait inside `{ }` the last command exit status matters. `grep ... || true` → exit 0 → then `exit 1`. Fine.\n\nActually simpler: just run testparm and if it fails, err with message. testparm prints its own error output to stderr which shows through:\n\n```bash\nreload_config() {\n    testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" >/dev/null || err \"smb.conf is invalid — fixes not loaded\"\n    if systemctl is-active --quiet smbd 2>/dev/null; then\n        sudo smbcontrol smbd reload-config\n        log \"smbd config reloaded\"\n    else\n        warn \"smbd not running — config validated but not loaded (enable with 'pos share smb-server enable')\"\n    fi\n}\n```\n\n`testparm -s \"$SMB_CONF\" >/dev/null` — testparm writes the parsed config to stdout and errors to stderr. With >/dev/null we keep stderr visible. On failure, `err` exits. \n\nNow — one subtlety about awk marker matching: the marker lines contain `$name`. If name contains regex chars — awk uses string equality `$0 == s` so no regex issue. Good. But name with spaces? Samba share names shouldn't have spaces. Validate name: `case \"$name\" in *[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*) err \"Invalid share name '$name'\" ;; esac`. Good for security (used in awk/markers).\n\nNow the client tool `bin/pos-share-smb-client`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n# POS: share smb-client — Mount SMB/CIFS shares (ephemeral or persistent systemd mount units)\n\nsource \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/common.sh\" 2>/dev/null || source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/common.sh\"\nsource \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/notify.sh\" 2>/dev/null || source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/notify.sh\"\n\ncommand -v mount.cifs &>/dev/null || err \"mount.cifs not found (install cifs-utils)\"\ncommand -v systemd-escape &>/dev/null || err \"systemd-escape not found\"\n\nusage() {\n    cat <<EOF\nUsage: pos share smb-client <command> [args]\n\nMount and manage SMB/CIFS shares from remote servers (cifs-utils).\n\nCommands:\n  mount <//server/share> <local-dir> [user]   One-shot mount (creates local-dir if needed)\n  unmount <local-dir>                          Unmount the share\n  list                                         Show active SMB mounts\n  persist <//server/share> <local-dir> [user]  Persistent mount via a systemd .mount unit\n                                               (automount — never blocks boot)\n  unpersist <local-dir>                        Stop, disable and remove the mount unit\n\nWith no user, guest access is attempted. With a user, you are prompted for\nthe Samba password (never stored; persistent mounts keep a chmod-600\ncredentials file at /etc/samba/credentials/).\n\nExamples:\n  pos share smb-client mount //100.100.100.1/media /mnt/smb/media\n  pos share smb-client persist //100.100.100.1/media /mnt/smb/media bob\n  pos share smb-client list\n  pos share smb-client unmount /mnt/smb/media\nEOF\n    exit 0\n}\n\ncmd=\"${1:-}\"\ncase \"$cmd\" in\n    -h|--help) usage ;;\n    mount|unmount|list|persist|unpersist) ;;\n    \"\") err \"Missing command (mount|unmount|list|persist|unpersist)\" ;;\n    *) err \"Unknown command '$cmd' (see --help)\" ;;\nesac\n\nvalidate_share() {\n    local what=\"$1\"\n    case \"$what\" in\n        //*) ;;\n        *) err \"Invalid share '$what' — expected <//server/share> (e.g. //10.0.0.5/media)\" ;;\n    esac\n}\n\nvalidate_dir() {\n    local where=\"$1\"\n    case \"$where\" in\n        /*) ;;\n        *) err \"Mount point must be an absolute path: $where\" ;;\n    esac\n}\n\n# Prompt for the Samba password (no echo) and return a path to a fresh\n# chmod-600 credentials file with username+password; caller removes it.\nmake_creds() {\n    local user=\"$1\" tmp\n    read -rsp \"Samba password for $user: \" pw </dev/tty || true\n    echo >&2\n    [ -n \"$pw\" ] || err \"empty password\"\n    tmp=\"$(mktemp)\"\n    chmod 600 \"$tmp\"\n    printf 'username=%s\\npassword=%s\\n' \"$user\" \"$pw\" > \"$tmp\"\n    printf '%s' \"$tmp\"\n}\n```\n\nHmm — `read -rsp ... </dev/tty || true`: reads from /dev/tty so tee doesn't swallow it. But the DEV.md says sudo reads /dev/tty. For our own prompt we should also read from /dev/tty? If run under the tee pipe, stdin is the pipe. `read -rsp` from stdin would read from the tee'd pipe → the tee holds a copy of stdin → the prompt could hang. Actually the INTERACTIVE_CMDS handling means the tool is NOT tee'd at all — pos runs it directly. So stdin is the real terminal. Let me check how other tools prompt: pos-communication-matrix-sender login uses `read -rsp \"Password for ${user}: \" password </dev/tty || true`. They explicitly use </dev/tty. I'll follow that pattern (read from /dev/tty) — consistent and safe even if not in INTERACTIVE_CMDS... but I AM adding to INTERACTIVE_CMDS. Both fine. Use `</dev/tty` like matrix-sender.\n\nWait — but if INTERACTIVE_CMDS is set, pos runs the tool with stdin attached (no tee). Then `</dev/tty` also works. OK.\n\nmount:\n```bash\n    mount)\n        what=\"${2:-}\"\n        where=\"${3:-}\"\n        user=\"${4:-}\"\n        [ -n \"$what\" ] && [ -n \"$where\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-client mount <//server/share> <local-dir> [user]\"\n        validate_share \"$what\"\n        validate_dir \"$where\"\n        sudo mkdir -p \"$where\"\n        if [ -n \"$user\" ]; then\n            creds=\"$(make_creds \"$user\")\"\n            trap 'rm -f \"$creds\"' EXIT\n            sudo mount.cifs \"$what\" \"$where\" -o \"credentials=$creds,uid=$(id -u),gid=$(id -g)\"\n        else\n            warn \"No user — attempting guest mount (works only if the server allows guest)\"\n            sudo mount.cifs \"$what\" \"$where\" -o \"guest,uid=$(id -u),gid=$(id -g)\"\n        fi\n        log \"Mounted $what at $where\"\n        ;;\n```\n\nWait — the trap: `trap 'rm -f \"$creds\"' EXIT` — creds is set. Fine. Note: mount.cifs needs root for the mount syscall — sudo. mount.cifs binary exists on the system at /sbin/mount.cifs. `sudo mount.cifs` works. Actually `sudo mount -t cifs` also works. Use `sudo mount -t cifs \"$what\" \"$where\" -o ...`. mount.cifs direct is fine too. I'll use `sudo mount -t cifs` for symmetry with nfs-client (`sudo mount -t nfs`).\n\nWith sudo mount -t cifs, options: `-o credentials=...,uid=...,gid=...`.\n\nunmount: `sudo umount \"$where\"` — mirror nfs.\n\nlist: `findmnt -t cifs`.\n\npersist:\n```bash\n    persist)\n        what=\"${2:-}\"\n        where=\"${3:-}\"\n        user=\"${4:-}\"\n        [ -n \"$what\" ] && [ -n \"$where\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-client persist <//server/share> <local-dir> [user]\"\n        validate_share \"$what\"\n        validate_dir \"$where\"\n        unit=\"$(systemd-escape --path --suffix=mount \"$where\")\"\n        unit_file=\"/etc/systemd/system/${unit}\"\n        opts=\"uid=$(id -u),gid=$(id -g),_netdev,noexec,x-systemd.automount\"\n        creds=\"\"\n        if [ -n \"$user\" ]; then\n            creds_file=\"/etc/samba/credentials/$(basename \"$where\")\"\n            sudo mkdir -p /etc/samba/credentials\n            tmp=\"$(make_creds \"$user\")\"\n            sudo install -m 600 \"$tmp\" \"$creds_file\"\n            rm -f \"$tmp\"\n            opts=\"credentials=$creds_file,$opts\"\n        else\n            opts=\"guest,$opts\"\n        fi\n        sudo mkdir -p \"$where\"\n        cat <<UNIT | sudo tee \"$unit_file\" >/dev/null\n[Unit]\nDescription=SMB mount of ${what} at ${where}\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Mount]\nWhat=${what}\nWhere=${where}\nType=cifs\nOptions=${opts}\nUNIT\n        sudo systemctl daemon-reload\n        sudo systemctl enable --now \"$unit\"\n        log \"Persistent SMB mount: ${what} → ${where} (${unit})\"\n        notify_send \"SMB mount persisted: ${what} → ${where}\"\n        ;;\n```\n\nWait — credentials file naming: `$(basename \"$where\")` might collide between different servers with same dir name. Use a safer name — hash or sanitized share name. Let me use `$(basename \"$where\")` → e.g. /mnt/smb/media → media. Collision unlikely for personal use. But better: use the share part of `what`: `what#*//` then after first `/`: `share_part=\"${what#*//}\"; share_part=\"${share_part#*/}\"` → e.g. //100.100.100.1/media → media. Same result. Keep basename of where — simple and stable (matches the mount unit name). Fine.\n\nx-systemd.automount: with automount, `enable --now` mounts on first access (not immediately). That's the boot-safe design. But `notify`/log says \"Persistent SMB mount\". The NFS tool enables --now and it mounts immediately. With automount, `systemctl enable --now unit` starts the automount unit — the mount unit is triggered by the automount. Actually with x-systemd.automount, systemd generates both <unit>.mount and <unit>.automount. `systemctl enable --now \"$unit\"` — enabling the .mount also enables the .automount? The automount is a separate unit <name>.automount. `systemctl enable --now mnt-smb-media.mount` — enables mount; automount auto-starts when mount is started? With x-systemd.automount option in the mount unit, systemd creates a paired automount unit and starting the mount unit actually starts the automount. Yes — the automount unit is automatically generated and `systemctl start mnt-x.mount` starts the automount instead (mounts on access). OK.\n\nHmm, wait: does `x-systemd.automount` need `nofail`? With automount, no boot block, nofail unnecessary. Good.\n\nunpersist:\n```bash\n    unpersist)\n        where=\"${2:-}\"\n        [ -n \"$where\" ] || err \"Usage: pos share smb-client unpersist <local-dir>\"\n        validate_dir \"$where\"\n        unit=\"$(systemd-escape --path --suffix=mount \"$where\")\"\n        unit_file=\"/etc/systemd/system/${unit}\"\n        if [ ! -f \"$unit_file\" ]; then\n            warn \"No systemd mount unit for $where (${unit})\"\n            exit 0\n        fi\n        sudo systemctl disable \"$unit\" 2>/dev/null || true\n        sudo systemctl stop \"$unit\" 2>/dev/null || true\n        sudo systemctl stop \"${unit%.mount}.automount\" 2>/dev/null || true\n        sudo rm -f \"$unit_file\"\n        sudo rm -f \"/etc/systemd/system/${unit%.mount}.automount\" 2>/dev/null || true\n        sudo rm -f \"/etc/samba/credentials/$(basename \"$where\")\"\n        sudo systemctl daemon-reload\n        log \"Removed persistent SMB mount: $where\"\n        notify_send \"SMB persistent mount removed: $where\"\n        ;;\n```\n\nThe automount unit file: when x-systemd.automount is set, systemd creates <escaped>.automount on the fly (it's synthesized from the mount unit options — no actual file). So no .automount file to remove. But `systemctl stop` of the .automount... the automount unit exists in the unit list dynamically. `sudo systemctl stop \"${unit%.mount}.automount\" 2>/dev/null || true` — safe. And no automount file rm needed. Let me drop the automount file rm but keep the stop (harmless). Actually to keep it simpler: stop mount + stop automount + rm mount file + rm creds + daemon-reload.\n\nHmm — `${unit%.mount}` — unit is like `mnt-smb-media.mount`. `${unit%.mount}` → `mnt-smb-media`. + `.automount` → `mnt-smb-media.automount`. Good.\n\nAlso remove empty credentials dir if 