e (honest critique):**\n\n1. **The `--help`-before-deps-guard ambiguity.** I hit a real judgment call: deps guards fire before `-h|--help` (unlike many CLI tools where help works without deps). I discovered the precedent by *reading the NFS tools*, not from any doc. The docs don't state whether `pos share nfs-server --help` works on a box without nfs-kernel-server. A doc line \"deps guards run before usage dispatch — help also errors without deps (matches existing tools)\" would have saved the check. Actually — I think DEV.md may mention that deps guards fire before usage; not sure. But there's a deeper gap: this behavior is arguably bad UX and the docs just encode the status quo without saying why. Could be improved by a note.\n\n2. **No explicit guidance on *test strategy without root/system deps*.** The tools need sudo and real samba to test end-to-end. The repo's test story for tools is thin — there's `make check` (syntax/exec-bit/dispatch smoke) but no pattern for behavior testing. I improvised the stub-PATH harness in /tmp. The docs say tools must be testable, but there's no documented pattern (\"how to test a tool that needs sudo/systemd\" — e.g. env-override patterns, stub PATH). That's a real gap. A short section in DEV.md on \"testing tools that need root/systemd/missing binaries\" with the env-override convention would make future tool work much faster.\n\n3. **Document map ranges in AGENT_Context are auto-generated — good, but the \"How to modify\" table could have covered \"adding a new tool\" more completely.** Actually I found the table good. Hmm.\n\n4. **AGENTS.md duplicates DEV.md a lot.** The Quick facts read like a compressed DEV.md. That's fine as a cheat sheet, but there's drift risk: if a convention changes, both files must change. I noticed the line-count rows (\"hand-maintained, not gen-checked\") are mentioned in AGENTS.md but the actual requirement (update the lib/common.sh row count) is obscure — I never had to do it because I didn't change lib/common.sh, but the wording \"the line-count rows above the filetable marker (e.g. lib/common.sh)\" is cryptic: what rows? It took AGENTS.md to tell me they're hand-maintained; a maintainer would want to know *which* rows and *when* to bump them. Could be clearer.\n\n5. **`pos share smb` two-token dispatch failure** — I hit `pos share smb` → \"unknown command 'share'\". Not a doc issue per se, but the docs' \"dispatches by longest-prefix arg matching\" line doesn't explain that `pos <cat> <partial>` doesn't fuzzy-match. Minor.\n\n6. **Missing: how to verify a tool that has no POS_FLAGS** — completions: I grepped completions/pos.bash for \"smb\" and found nothing, then had to reason that subcommand-style tools have no completion entries. The docs say POS_FLAGS feed tab-completion, but don't say subcommand-style tools (like nfs/smb client/server) produce no flag completions — which is correct behavior, just not stated. Minor.\n\n7. **Testing the actual regex/awk details**: not a doc thing.\n\n8. **One more real one: the docs don't tell you what \"clean\" means for the final commit** — i.e., no explicit checklist for what a committed feature must include (tool + tests + docs rows + TODO move + commit message style). DEV.md checklists partially do this (\"Adding a new Feature/App/Tool\"). I found them decent. Could be strengthened with the concrete file list per category (bin/pos EXAMPLES + INTERACTIVE_CMDS + preinstall PACKAGES + POS.md + howto + AGENT_Context rows + AGENT_TODO Done).\n\nLet me also think about the biggest actual frictions I experienced:\n\n- The awk bug (inblock marker eaten) — that was a code bug, not a doc gap. But the *convention* of marker blocks is undocumented! The SMB tools introduced a new pattern (managed marker blocks in a config file with awk block deletion). If a future agent adds a similar \"managed block\" tool, the pattern won't be discoverable. A one-line note in DEV.md about the marker-block idiom with the `inblock==1` guard would encode the hard-won lesson. That's a genuine \"would you make it better\" answer.\n\n- The docmap in AGENT_Context: auto-generated ranges — great. But when I needed to add a Common Tasks row, I had to find the right section; docmap made that easy. Good.\n\n- **Rate the docs**: Overall: 8/10 for a code-generating agent workflow. They were decisive in: header format, gen/check gate, INTERACTIVE_CMDS, PACKAGES vs command -v, hand-maintained vs generated split, AGENT_TODO. The gaps: (1) deps-guard-before-help behavior not stated (I had to infer from tools); (2) no documented behavior-testing pattern for root/systemd/missing-deps tools (I improvised stubs); (3) marker-block/managed-block idiom not documented (new pattern, no precedent record); (4) duplication/drift risk between AGENTS.md and DEV.md; (5) filetable line-count rows instruction is cryptic.\n\nWould I make them better? Yes — concrete suggestions:\n1. DEV.md: add \"Testing tools that need root / systemd / absent binaries\" — the env-override + stub-PATH pattern (SMB_CONF, SMB_CREDS_DIR, UNIT_DIR, FLAGS_DIR precedent).\n2. DEV.md: document the managed-marker-block config idiom (start/end markers, awk with inblock guard) so future tools reuse it.\n3. AGENTS.md or DEV.md: state explicitly that deps guards run before usage()/help dispatch (so --help also errors) — remove the guesswork.\n4. AGENTS.md: clarify the \"line-count rows above the filetable marker\" — say which file/rows (e.g. `lib/common.sh` row in DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md line-count table) and when to bump.\n5. Consider one canonical conventions doc with AGENTS.md pointing at it (reduce drift), or a doc-sync check that flags duplication. Actually the repo deliberately has AGENTS.md as the agent-facing distilled version — that's fine; just add a note \"if you change a convention, update both DEV.md and this Quick facts list\".\n6. Completion note: state that subcommand-style tools (no POS_FLAGS) intentionally produce no flag-completion entries.\n7.