            "output": "<path>/mnt/hdd/ciya_space/work/repo/Linux_post_install/DOC/DEV.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Development Guide\n2: \n3: How this repo works, how to add features, and what to keep in mind when editing.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## Architecture\n8: \n9: ### Installation Phases\n10: \n11: ```\n12:                   install.sh\n13:                       │\n14:           ┌───────────┼───────────┐\n15:           ▼           ▼           ▼\n16:     preinstall.sh   bin/*    postinstall.sh\n17:     (packages)   → /usr/local/bin  (config + services)\n18: ```\n19: \n20: | Phase | Script | Responsibility |\n21: |-------|--------|----------------|\n22: | Pre | `preinstall.sh` | System packages, apt repos, global binaries (yt-dlp) |\n23: | Install | `install.sh` | Copies `bin/*` → `/usr/local/bin/` (chmod 755), `lib/common.sh` + `lib/flags.sh` + `lib/entertainment-lib.sh` → `/usr/local/bin/` (chmod 644) |\n24: | Post | `postinstall.sh` | User config (SSH keys, PATH, bash completion), systemd services |\n25: \n26: Each phase is independent and runs only if the corresponding script exists.\n27: \n28: ### Directory Layout\n29: \n30: | Directory | Purpose | Installed To |\n31: |-----------|---------|-------------|\n32: | `bin/` | Daily-use CLI tools and wrappers | `/usr/local/bin/` |\n33: | `apps/<category>/` | Optional desktop app installers | run on demand |\n34: | `lib/` | Shared libraries: `common.sh` (helpers), `flags.sh` (feature flags), `notify.sh` (multi-platform alerting), `entertainment-lib.sh` (entertainment scheduling) | sourced at build time |\n35: | `config/` | Gitignored user config files | `~/.config/<app>/` (via postinstall) |\n36: | `entertainment/` | Public-API plugins for the entertainment module | `/usr/local/bin` (via install.sh Phase 2) |\n37: | `compose/` | ScaleTail templates (git submodule) | `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail` |\n38: | `systemd/` | Systemd unit files | `/etc/systemd/system/` (via postinstall) |\n39: \n40: ### The `pos` CLI\n41: \n42: `bin/pos` is a smart dispatcher. It scans its own directory for executable `pos-*` files and uses variable-length argument matching:\n43: \n44: ```\n45: pos docker compose up jellyfin\n46:   → tries pos-docker-compose-up-jellyfin  (not found)\n47:   → tries pos-docker-compose-up           (not found)\n48:   → finds pos-docker-compose              → runs with args \"up jellyfin\"\n49: ```\n50: \n51: All non-interactive commands log to `~/.local/share/linux_post_install/logs/`.\n52: \n53: `pos help <full command>` shows a tool's help, e.g. `pos help communication telegram-sender` (all words joined with dashes → `pos-communication-telegram-sender --help`). `pos <category>` or `pos <category> --help` shows a category's subcommands (derived from the `pos-<category>-*` filenames in `bin/` — no script execution, so it works even for root-only/interactive tools like `system-firewall`).\n54: \n55: **When adding a command, `bin/pos` itself has one thing to keep in sync:**\n56: \n57: - **The usage text** (`usage()` function) — the CATEGORIES block is **auto-derived** from the `pos-*` filenames in `bin/` (no manual edit, can't drift). The EXAMPLES block is the only hand-maintained part: add a line there only if you want the tool showcased in `pos --help`.\n58: - **`INTERACTIVE_CMDS`** (space-separated list above the dispatch loop) — commands that **read stdin** (password prompts, selection menus: `system-firewall`, `media-mp4`, `system-backup`, `share-usb-server`, `communication-telegram-listener`) must be added here. Everything else is piped through `tee` for logging, which would hang or swallow an interactive prompt. sudo's own password prompt is unaffected — it reads from `/dev/tty`. Trade-off: it's all-or-nothing **per script** — adding a flag-style tool with *any* prompting subcommand (e.g. `share-usb-server --share`) means *every* subcommand of that script skips output logging (e.g. `pos share usb server --ls` loses the `tee` log too).\n59: \n60: ### Shared Library (`lib/common.sh`)\n61: \n62: Sourced by most scripts. Key functions:\n63: \n64: | Function | Purpose |\n65: |----------|---------|\n66: | `log \"msg\"` | Green `[+]` status message |\n67: | `warn \"msg\"` | Yellow `[!]` warning |\n68: | `err \"msg\"` | Red `ERROR:` + exit 1 |\n69: | `ok \"msg\"` | Green `OK` prefix |\n70: | `section \"title\"` | Cyan-bordered section header |\n71: | `step N T \"msg\"` | Numbered step header |\n72: | `run cmd` | Executes command, respects `$DRY_RUN` |\n73: | `spawn \"msg\" cmd` | Animated braille spinner + elapsed time |\n74: | `timer_start` / `timer_stop` | Elapsed time tracking |\n75: | `confirm \"prompt\"` | y/N prompt with optional default |\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## Adding a New CLI Tool\n80: \n81: Start from the template: `cp templates/pos-tool.sh bin/pos-<category>-<command> && chmod +x bin/pos-<category>-<command>`.\n82: \n83: ### 1. Create the script\n84: \n85: ```bash\n86: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n87: \n88: set -euo pipefail\n89: \n90: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/common.sh\"\n91: \n92: usage() {\n93:     cat <<EOF\n94: Usage: my-tool <argument>\n95: EOF\n96:     exit 0\n97: }\n98: \n99: case \"${1:-}\" in\n100:     -h|--help|\"\") usage ;;\n101: esac\n102: \n103: # --- script logic ---\n104: ```\n105: \n106: **Conventions:**\n107: - Shebang: `#!/usr/bin/env bash`\n108: - Strict mode: `set -euo pipefail`\n109: - `--help` flag: accept `-h` / `--help` via `case` pattern\n110: - Shared library: always source `common.sh` for colors, logging, spinners\n111: - Exit codes: `0` success, `1` error\n112: - No shared lib? Inline fallbacks:\n113:   ```bash\n114:   log()  { echo \"[+] $*\"; }\n115:   warn() { echo \"[!] $*\"; }\n116:   err()  { echo \"ERROR: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n117:   ```\n118:   If you skip `common.sh`, `make gen` adds the tool to the \"Scripts that do NOT source common.sh\" list in `DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md` automatically.\n119: \n120: ### 2. Make it discoverable\n121: \n122: - The dispatcher auto-discovers executable `bin/pos-*` files — no registration needed. The file **must be executable** (`chmod +x`, committed as mode `100755`); the dispatcher and `install.sh` skip non-executables.\n123: - `pos <category> --help` (and bare `pos <category>`) is derived from the `pos-<category>-*` filenames too — a new tool appears in its category's help automatically, with no registration (see [The `pos` CLI](#the-pos-cli)).\n124: - **Add the `# POS:` header** (single source of truth for the docs) right after the shebang/strict-mode lines:\n125:   ```bash\n126:   # POS: <category> <command> — one-line description rendered by `make gen`\n127:   # POS_FLAGS: --flag1 --flag2      # ONLY for flag-style tools\n128:   # POS_SUBCMDS: sub1 sub2          # ONLY for multi-command tools\n129:   ```\n130:   The description feeds the dispatch table, bin tree and file table in `DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md`; `POS_FLAGS` feeds flag completion and `POS_SUBCMDS` feeds subcommand completion in `completions/pos.bash` (both update via `make gen`). `make gen` only reads the text after the first `— ` — the `<category> <command>` words before it are convention-only (for nested tools, keep the full path there, e.g. `# POS: communication telegram-listener — …`).\n131: - **Category-less vs categorized:** most tools are `bin/pos-<category>-<command>`. Use category-less `bin/pos-<cmd>` (e.g. `pos-config`, `pos-tree`) only for dispatcher/dev-level commands that fit no category — they dispatch and document like any tool but show with an empty category in the generated tables.\n132: - Nested tools (e.g. `bin/pos-communication-telegram-listener`) are auto-detected from filenames: the trailing segment (`listener`) is offered as a subcommand of the parent tool (`communication-telegram`) in `pos <category> --help` and tab-completion, instead of appearing as a flat sibling (`telegram-listener`). The flat dash-form (`pos communication telegram-listener`) still dispatches.\n133: - Optionally add an EXAMPLES line in `bin/pos` `usage()` to showcase the tool in `pos --help`.\n134: - If the command **reads stdin** (prompts/selection), add it to `INTERACTIVE_CMDS` in `bin/pos` — see [The `pos` CLI](#the-pos-cli).\n135: \n136: ### 3. Add system dependencies\n137: \n138: Add package names to the `PACKAGES` array in `preinstall.sh`:\n139: \n140: ```bash\n141: PACKAGES=(\n142:     ...\n143:     your-package\n144: )\n145: ```\n146: \n147: **Not in apt?** If the dependency ships as a manual installer (no package — e.g. `usbsrv`, the USB Redirector server), do **not** put it in `PACKAGES` (that would break `preinstall.sh`). Instead, add a `command -v <binary> || err \"… install from <URL>\"` guard in the tool itself and note the manual install in `usage()`/`DOC/POS.md`.\n148: \n149: ### 4. Config files (if needed)\n150: \n151: Two kinds of config, don't mix them up:\n152: \n153: - **Machine defaults shipped by the installer:** place the file in `config/` and add copy logic to `postinstall.sh`. If it contains secrets, add to `.gitignore` and document in `DOC/`.\n154: - **Runtime tool config set by the user:** `~/.config/linux_post_install/<tool>.env` with `chmod 600`. Load it with env-var precedence (flags > environment > file). Patterns: `pos-docker-compose` (`compose.env`), `pos-communication-telegram-sender` (`telegram.env`, edited via `pos config telegram` — token masked), and the shared ones below. Never store tokens in the repo.\n155:   - `system.env` — shared \"system\" settings loaded by `pos-system-*` tools via `load_system_env()` in `lib/common.sh` (currently `BACKUP_SERVICE_ROOTS`, `HEALTH_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_DAYS`). Env already exported wins over the file.\n156:   - `notify.env` — alerting platform selection (`NOTIFY_PLATFORM=telegram,matrix`), read by `lib/notify.sh`.\n157: \n158: ### 5. Add SSH keys (if needed)\n159: \n160: Place public keys in `config/authorized_keys` (one per line). `postinstall.sh` reads this file automatically.\n161: \n162: ### 6. Update the docs\n163: \n164: - `DOC/POS.md`: add the command to the section table + a detail block (commands, behavior, configuration). This is the one hand-written doc.\n165: - `DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md` generated sections (bin tree, dispatch table, no-common.sh list, line-count table) and the `completions/pos.bash` flags block are produced by `make gen` — do **not** hand-edit between the `GEN:START`/`GEN:END` markers.\n166: - Root `README.md`: only if the `pos` category list in the help text changes.\n167: \n168: ### 7. Test\n169: \n170: ```bash\n171: chmod +x bin/your-tool\n172: bash -n bin/your-tool\n173: shellcheck bin/your-tool\n174: ./bin/your-tool --help\n175: bin/pos help <full command>   # confirm dispatch works\n176: bin/pos <category> --help     # confirm category listing includes the new tool (first tool in a new category)\n177: make gen                      # regenerate doc tables + completion flags\n178: make check                    # full self-consistency gate (syntax, exec bits, doc/code sync, smoke)\n179: ```\n180: \n181: `make check` is the definition of done — the same check runs as a pre-commit hook once you've run `make hook`.\n182: \n183: ---\n184: \n185: ## Adding an Entertainment Plugin\n186: \n187: The `entertainment` module routes public-API data to Telegram via the single runner `pos entertainment send <plugin>` (`bin/pos-entertainment-send`). Auto-triggering is config-driven: `ENABLED` in `entertainment.env` holds `plugin, interval` pairs; the tools `pos entertainment config|enable|disable|status` (`bin/pos-entertainment-*`) reconcile the schedule. All shared logic (ENABLED parsing, interval→schedule mapping, scheduler sync) lives in `lib/entertainment-lib.sh` — sourced by the `pos-entertainment-*` tools (never by plugins). The scheduler is **systemd user timers** — the only backend (requires a reachable user systemd manager).\n188: \n189: ### 1. Create the plugin\n190: \n191: Drop an executable script in `entertainment/<name>.sh` with a `# POS_PLUGIN: <name>` marker on line 3 (this is what makes it a plugin — the installed runner lists plugins by this marker, not by `.sh` files, since `/usr/local/bin` is shared with other tooling). Declare every config key the plugin reads with `# POS_KEYS: <KEY> <description> (required|optional)` lines right after it — `pos entertainment config` prints these in its Keys section and uses them to warn/not-warn on `config set`:\n192: \n193: ```bash\n194: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n195: set -euo pipefail\n196: # POS_PLUGIN: myplugin\n197: # POS_KEYS: MYPLUGIN_URL <feed url> (required)\n198: # POS_KEYS: MYPLUGIN_TAG <filter tag> (optional)\n199: err() { echo \"ERROR: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n200: \n201: command -v curl &>/dev/null || err \"curl not found\"\n202: \n203: data=\"$(curl -fsS --max-time 20 https://api.example.com/foo)\"\n204: printf 'Title: %s\\n' \"$data\"\n205: ```\n206: \n207: **Contract:** plugins are **self-contained** — do **not** source `lib/common.sh`. Its `log`/`warn`/`ok` helpers print to **stdout**, and the runner captures stdout as the message to send (helper chatter would be sent to Telegram). All stdout is the message; errors go to stderr and exit nonzero. Plugins must be non-interactive (no prompts) — the module is designed for systemd user timers.\n208: \n209: ### 2. Config (if needed)\n210: \n211: Read runtime values from `~/.config/linux_post_install/entertainment.env` (chmod 600, env precedence) — same pattern as `telegram.env`. Example: `weather.sh` uses `WEATHER_LAT`/`WEATHER_LON`. Declare each key with a `# POS_KEYS:` header line (see step 1) so `pos entertainment config` lists it and `config set` recognizes it.\n212: \n213: ### 3. Deps\n214: \n215: `curl` and `jq` are already in `preinstall.sh` PACKAGES. Anything else: guard with `command -v … || err \"…\"` and, if apt-available, add to PACKAGES.\n216: \n217: ### 4. Done\n218: \n219: Plugins are not `pos-*` tools, so `make gen`/`make check` don't scan them — verify with `bash -n entertainment/<name>.sh` and a live `pos entertainment send <name> --print` run. Document the plugin in `DOC/POS.md`'s entertainment plugin table.\n220: \n221: ---\n222: \n223: ## Adding an Optional App\n224: \n225: Start from the template: `cp templates/app.sh apps/<category>/<name>.sh`.\n226: \n227: ### 1. Create the installer\n228: \n229: ```bash\n230: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n231: set -euo pipefail\n232: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../../lib/common.sh\"\n233: \n234: install_myapp() {\n235:     command -v myapp &>/dev/null && { log \"myapp already installed\"; return 0; }\n236:     spawn \"Installing myapp\" sudo apt install -y myapp\n237: }\n238: \n239: uninstall_myapp() {\n240:     command -v myapp &>/dev/null || { log \"myapp not installed\"; return 0; }\n241:     spawn \"Removing myapp\" sudo apt purge -y myapp\n242:     spawn \"Cleaning up dependencies\" sudo apt autoremove -y\n243: }\n244: \n245: case \"${1:-}\" in\n246:     uninstall) uninstall_myapp ;;\n247:     *) install_myapp ;;\n248: esac\n249: ```\n250: \n251: Place it in `apps/<category>/<name>.sh`. It auto-appears in the picker — no registration needed.\n252: \n253: **Categories:** `browsers`, `development`, `media`, `networking`, `remote-access`, `system`, `utilities`\n254: \n255: **Docs:** add a row to the catalog table in `DOC/APPS.md` (name, category, purpose, install method).\n256: \n257: ### 2. Conventions\n258: \n259: - Idempotent: check `command -v` (or `flatpak list` / file existence) before installing **and** uninstalling\n260: - Every app **must** provide an `uninstall_<name>()` function and dispatch on `uninstall` via the `case` above — `apps/install.sh --uninstall` depends on it\n261: - APT packages → `sudo apt install -y` inside `spawn`, remove with `sudo apt purge -y` + `sudo apt autoremove -y`\n262: - Repo-based apps (apt repo added at install) → also remove the `.list` file and keyring in uninstall\n263: - Official scripts → `curl ... | sh` inside `spawn`\n264: - Flatpak → `flatpak install -y flathub <app-id>` inside `spawn`, remove with `flatpak uninstall -y <app-id>`\n265: - `.deb` files → download to temp, `sudo apt install -y ./file.deb` inside `spawn`\n266: - File/AppImage installs → remove the installed files, symlinks, and desktop entries in uninstall\n267: - `usermod` for groups → print re-login reminder\n268: \n269: ---\n270: \n271: ## Editing an Existing Tool\n272: \n273: 1. Find the script in `bin/`\n274: 2. Understand its contract (args, output, exit codes)\n275: 3. Make the change — keep it idempotent\n276: 4. Update `DOC/POS.md` (or the relevant doc) if behaviour changed\n277: 5. Run `shellcheck` on the modified file\n278: \n279: ---\n280: \n281: ## Best Practices\n282: \n283: ### Alerting\n284: \n285: To notify on events, source the shared helper instead of calling a platform tool directly:\n286: \n287: ```bash\n288: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/notify.sh\" 2>/dev/null || source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/notify.sh\"\n289: notify_send \"Backup completed\"\n290: notify_send \"**disk full**\" --markdown\n291: ```\n292: \n293: `notify_send` is deliberately dependency-free (defines only itself, so it never clobbers a tool's own `log`/`warn`/`err`) and **silent-fails**: if no platform is configured it warns and returns 0, never breaking the caller's flow or exit code. Source it opt-in in any tool that should alert; for failure alerts use `trap 'notify_send \"...\" ERR'`.\n294: \n295: **Multi-platform routing:** `notify_send` delivers to every platform listed in `NOTIFY_PLATFORM` (env or `~/.config/linux_post_install/notify.env`, default `telegram`, comma-separated to send to all). Adding a new platform (e.g. Matrix/Synapse) means creating a `bin/pos-communication-<platform>` tool that implements the **sender contract**:\n296: \n297: ```bash\n298: pos-communication-<platform> send <value> [--markdown]   # exit 0 on delivery\n299: ```\n300: \n301: then listing it in `NOTIFY_PLATFORM`. Platform keys map to tool names via `notify_sender_name()` in `lib/notify.sh` — the telegram platform key stays `telegram` but its tool is `pos-communication-telegram-sender`. `pos-communication-telegram-sender` already follows this (`--markdown` is an alias for `--parse-mode markdown`). No changes to `lib/notify.sh` are needed for a new platform.\n302: \n303: ### Idempotency\n304: \n305: Check before creating, use `>>` with grep guards, don't overwrite user configs.\n306: \n307: ### Error Handling\n308: \n309: ```bash\n310: set -euo pipefail\n311: command -v docker &>/dev/null || { echo \"docker not found\"; exit 1; }\n312: [[ -n \"${1:-}\" ]] || { echo \"Usage: my-tool <arg>\"; exit 1; }\n313: ```\n314: \n315: ### Portability\n316: \n317: Targets **Debian** and **Ubuntu**. Use `apt`, assume bash at `/usr/bin/env bash`, check tools with `command -v`.\n318: \n319: ### Dry-run Support\n320: \n321: Scripts support `--dry-run`. Use the `run()` helper:\n322: \n323: ```bash\n324: run() {\n325:     if [ \"$DRY_RUN\" -eq 1 ]; then\n326:         log \"(dry-run) $*\"\n327:     else\n328:         \"$@\"\n329:     fi\n330: }\n331: run sudo apt install -y git\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ### Security\n335: \n336: - Never hardcode secrets — put them in `config/` (gitignored) or, for runtime tool config, `~/.config/linux_post_install/<tool>.env`\n337: - `chmod 600` for sensitive files\n338: - Mask secrets in `config` output (see `pos-communication-telegram-sender`'s `mask_token`)\n339: - Validate input before shell commands\n340: - Use `sudo` only where needed\n341: \n342: ### Naming\n343: \n344: - CLI tools: `bin/pos-<category>-<command>`\n345: - Legacy wrappers: `bin/wr-*`\n346: - App installers: `apps/<category>/<name>.sh`\n347: - Features: `features/<name>.sh`\n348: - Lowercase with hyphens\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## Features & Flags\n353: \n354: `features/` holds scripts the user is likely to customize (e.g. `autostart.sh`). Unlike `bin/` (synced on every install), features are installed on demand and **never overwritten without asking**.\n355: \n356: ### Adding a Feature\n357: \n358: Start from the template: `cp templates/feature.sh features/<name>.sh`.\n359: \n360: 1. Create `features/<name>.sh` following the CLI tool template (shebang, `set -euo pipefail`, `--help`).\n361: 2. Nothing else is registered — `./install.sh --feature` auto-discovers it, copies it to `/usr/local/bin/`, asks before overwriting an existing file, and sets its flag.\n362: 3. If the feature backs a systemd service, gate the service on the flag in `postinstall.sh` (see below).\n363: \n364: ### Flag System\n365: \n366: System-wide flag store at `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/flags/` (presence = set, content = optional value). Sourced via `lib/flags.sh` (or the installed `/usr/local/bin/flags.sh`):\n367: \n368: ```bash\n369: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/lib/flags.sh\" 2>/dev/null || source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/flags.sh\"\n370: \n371: flag_set autostart        # green flag\n372: flag_set app \"2.1\"        # green flag with a value\n373: flag_is_set autostart     # test (0/1) — the primitive consumers use\n374: flag_value app            # → \"2.1\"\n375: flag_list                 # names of all set flags\n376: flag_clear autostart\n377: ```\n378: \n379: Writes use `run` + `sudo`, so they respect `--dry-run`. CLI equivalents: `flag-reader`, `flag-set`, `flag-clear`.\n380: \n381: **Example — service gated on a flag** (in `postinstall.sh`'s systemd loop):\n382: \n383: ```bash\n384: if [ \"$svc_name\" = \"myapp.service\" ] && ! flag_is_set myapp; then\n385:     warn \"myapp feature not installed — skipping myapp.service\"\n386:     continue\n387: fi\n388: ```\n389: \n390: ---\n391: \n392: ## Working with Systemd\n393: \n394: Create `systemd/<name>.service` — `postinstall.sh` copies it to `/etc/systemd/system/` and enables it automatically.\n395: \n396: ```ini\n397: [Unit]\n398: Description=My Service\n399: After=network-online.target\n400: Wants=network-online.target\n401: \n402: [Service]\n403: Type=simple\n404: ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/your-script.sh\n405: Restart=on-failure\n406: RestartSec=10\n407: \n408: [Install]\n409: WantedBy=multi-user.target\n410: ```\n411: \n412: ---\n413: \n414: ## Working with Config Files\n415: \n416: 1. Place the file in `config/`\n417: 2. Add copy logic to `postinstall.sh`:\n418: \n419: ```bash\n420: if [ -f config/your-config.conf ]; then\n421:     mkdir -p \"$HOME/.config/your-app\"\n422:     cp config/your-config.conf \"$HOME/.config/your-app/your-config.conf\"\n423:     chmod 600 \"$HOME/.config/your-app/your-config.conf\"\n424: fi\n425: ```\n426: \n427: ---\n428: \n429: ## Docker Compose / ScaleTail\n430: \n431: The installer clones [ScaleTail](https://github.com/tailscale-dev/ScaleTail) templates to `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail/` — 119+ self-hosted services with a Tailscale sidecar pattern. Each service gets a `tail-xxxxx.ts.net` URL via `network_mode: service:tailscale`.\n432: \n433: ### Config Strategy — Three Layers\n434: \n435: Values cascade from least to most specific:\n436: \n437: ```\n438: Template .env          (per-service defaults from ScaleTail)\n439:        ↓\n440: Global config          (~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env)\n441:        ↓\n442: Per-service .env       (/srv/<service>/.env) — created on first deploy, NEVER overwritten\n443: ```\n444: \n445: On first `pos docker compose up <service>`:\n446: 1. Template `.env` is copied to `/srv/<service>/.env`\n447: 2. Matching keys from global config are filled in\n448: 3. If `TS_AUTHKEY` is still empty, you're prompted to enter it\n449: 4. After that, the per-service `.env` is **never touched** — not even by `update`\n450: \n451: ### Layout\n452: \n453: | Path | Purpose | Mutability |\n454: |------|---------|------------|\n455: | `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail/services/<name>/` | ScaleTail templates (git repo) | Read-only |\n456: | `~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env` | Your global defaults | Edit via `config set` or `config edit` |\n457: | `/srv/<service>/` | Active deployment | Per-service `.env` preserved forever |\n458: \n459: ### Key Commands\n460: \n461: | Command | Behaviour |\n462: |---------|-----------|\n463: | `pos docker compose up <service>` | Deploys to `$SERVICES_BASE/<service>/`, creates `config/` + `data/`, generates `.env` from global defaults |\n464: | `pos docker compose down <service>` | Stops the stack |\n465: | `pos docker compose update` | `git pull` templates + refreshes `compose.yaml` for all deployed services (`.env` untouched) |\n466: | `pos docker compose config set K=V` | Sets a global default in `~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env` |\n467: | `pos docker compose config show` | Displays current global config and `SERVICES_BASE` |\n468: | `pos docker compose config edit` | Opens global config in `$EDITOR` |\n469: \n470: ### Global Config Keys\n471: \n472: | Key | Required | Default | Purpose |\n473: |-----|----------|---------|---------|\n474: | `TS_AUTHKEY` | Yes | — | Tailscale auth key for sidecar networking |\n475: | `TZ` | No | `Europe/Amsterdam` | Timezone for services |\n476: | `DNS_SERVER` | No | `9.9.9.9` | Custom DNS server |\n477: | `SERVICES_BASE` | No | `/srv` | Root directory for all deployments |\n478: \n479: ---\n480: \n481: ## Commit Guidelines\n482: \n483: - Use conventional prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`\n484: - Explain *why*, not just *what*\n485: - One logical change per commit\n486: \n487: ```\n488: feat: add pos-disk-usage for monitoring disk space\n489: fix: pos-network-ip fails when no default route exists\n490: docs: add example output for pos-network-scan\n491: ```\n492: \n493: ---\n494: \n495: ## Useful Commands\n496: \n497: ```bash\n498: # Syntax check a single script\n499: bash -n bin/my-script\n500: \n501: # ShellCheck linting\n502: shellcheck bin/my-script\n503: \n504: # Check all scripts\n505: for f in bin/* apps/*/*.sh lib/common.sh install.sh preinstall.sh postinstall.sh; do\n506:     bash -n \"$f\" || echo \"FAIL: $f\"\n507: done\n508: \n509: # Init submodule\n510: git submodule update --init\n511: \n512: # Pull latest ScaleTail templates\n513: git submodule update --remote compose/scale-tail\n514: \n515: # Test install in Docker\n516: docker run --rm -it -v $PWD:/repo ubuntu:22.04 bash\n517: # inside: cd /repo && ./install.sh\n518: \n519: # Test app installers\n520: ./apps/install.sh\n521: ./apps/install.sh --all\n522: ./apps/install.sh docker vscode\n523: ```\n\n(End of file - total 523 lines)\n</content>",
