ght call. But it complicates tests (systemctl --user stubs). The stub-PATH pattern handles that.\n\n   Actually, let me reconsider scope to keep it shippable. Commands:\n   - `pos network download start` — ensure daemon running (install/enable user unit, start it)\n   - `pos network download stop` — stop daemon\n   - `pos network download status` — daemon + active downloads (aria2.getGlobalStat + tellActive)\n   - `pos network download add <url> [<url>...] [--dir <path>] [--out <name>] [--split <n>]` — enqueue downloads, prints GID(s). Also handles .torrent/magnet (aria2.addUri handles http/ftp; torrents need aria2.addTorrent with the file content, or addUri with magnet).\n   - `pos network download list` — list downloads (tellStatus all) with state (active/waiting/paused/error/complete), progress %, speed, ETA.\n   - `pos network download pause <gid|all>` / `resume <gid|all>` / `remove <gid|all>` — queue control.\n   - `pos network download watch [--dir]`? Maybe a simple progress view. Could skip.\n\n   What about one-shot convenience? `pos network download <url>` as bare command → add and watch? Or keep everything under subcommands. Given daemon model, `add` is the core. Maybe make bare `pos network download <url>` an alias for `add` + wait? Could be nice but let me not overload. Keep explicit subcommands.\n\n   Magnet links: `aria2.addUri` works with magnet: URIs. Torrent files: need `aria2.addTorrent` with base64 content — the tool reads the file and base64-encodes it. Doable with base64.\n\n   JSON-RPC over HTTP: use `curl -s http://localhost:6800/jsonrpc -d '...'`. curl is a core dep (already used in matrix/telegram senders). JSON parsing: jq? Is jq a dep? Let me check if the repo uses jq anywhere. Probably. Let me check.\n\n2. **Dir/options defaults:** `--dir` default `$HOME/Downloads`. Runtime config dir `~/.config/linux_post_install/` for service file? Service file is user unit dir. Actually config: `~/.config/linux_post_install/download.env` (per repo convention — runtime tool config is `~/.config/linux_post_install/<tool>.env`, chmod 600, env precedence). Could hold RPC port, but default 6800 is fine.\n\n3. **Deps:** `aria2` (PACKAGES in preinstall.sh), plus `jq` if needed for JSON parsing (check if repo already uses jq; else parse with grep/sed or python). Let me check jq usage. curl is surely used already.\n\n4. **INTERACTIVE_CMDS:** no stdin reads planned → not needed.\n\n5. **Test seams (env overrides):** \n   - `DOWNLOAD_DIR` (default `$HOME/Downloads`) — the default download dir.\n   - `RPC_PORT` (default 6800) — the JSON-RPC port; also used to set `--rpc-listen-port` in the unit.\n   - `USER_UNIT_DIR` (default `~/.config/systemd/user`) — where the unit is written (SMB client precedent: UNIT_DIR).\n   - Stub PATH for `systemctl`, `aria2c`, `curl`, `jq`.\n   \n   Hmm, for testing the JSON-RPC client we'd stub curl to echo canned responses. That's the stub-PATH pattern.\n\n6. **systemd user service unit content:**\n   ```\n   [Unit]\n   Description=linux-post-install aria2 RPC daemon\n   After=network-online.target\n   \n   [Service]\n   Type=simple\n   ExecStart=/usr/bin/aria2c --enable-rpc --rpc-listen-port=%i --rpc-allow-origin-all --dir=%h/Downloads --continue=true --max-connection-per-server=16 --split=16 --seed-time=0\n   Restart=on-failure\n   \n   [Install]\n   WantedBy=default.target\n   ```\n   Hmm, `--seed-time=0` means don't seed (stop after download complete)? For a downloader, seeding by default is not desired; user can change. Actually aria2 default seed ratio... default `--seed-time` is 120 minutes for bittorrent. For a download tool, we might set `--seed-time=0` to stop after download. Or make seeding opt-in via a flag `--seed`. Keep service default `--seed-time=0`, and `add` has no seed flag... but torrent users might want to seed. Let me add service option via env override instead. Keep simple: service uses `--seed-time=0`; note in howto how to change.\n\n   Wait — one consideration: `--rpc-secret`? aria2 RPC on localhost with no auth is a mild risk (any local user could drive it). Using `--rpc-secret=<token>` would require the tool to know the token. The tool generates one at first start and stores it in the env file (chmod 600). That matches the repo's security-consciousness (creds chmod 600, secrets never committed). I'll include a generated `RPC_SECRET` stored in `~/.config/linux_post_install/download.env` (chmod 600) and all requests send `token:<secret>`. \n\n   Also `--rpc-listen-all=false` (default localhost only). Good.\n\n7. **JSON parsing:** check jq availability in repo. Let me grep.\n\nLet me check jq and curl usage in repo, and how matrix-sender does JSON. Also check systemd user unit precedent (SMB used root units via sudo). And check whether any tool uses systemctl --user.\n\nLet me also check AGENT_Context \"How to modify\" section for adding tool requirements, and POS.md network table format (already seen). \n\nLet me run a couple more greps.",
