cmd_limit / cmd_set as designed.\n\ncmd_watch as designed:\n```bash\ncmd_watch() {\n    local gid=\"${1:-}\"\n    printf '\\033[?25l'\n    trap 'printf \"\\033[?25h\"; exit 0' INT TERM EXIT\n    if [ -n \"$gid\" ]; then\n        while :; do\n            local row\n            row=$(rpc aria2.tellStatus \"$gid\" | jq -r '... | @tsv' 2>/dev/null || true)\n            local st; st=$(printf '%s' \"$row\" | cut -f1)\n            printf '\\r\\033[K'\n            if [ -n \"$row\" ]; then\n                printf '%s' \"$row\" | awk -F'\\t' \"$ROW_AWK\" '{ printf \"  %-8s %-9s %6.1f%%  %s / %s  dl %s  up %s  conn %s  eta %s\", $1, $2, $3, $4, $5, hs($6), hs($7), $8, et($9) }'\n            fi\n            case \"$st\" in\n                complete|error|removed) printf '\\n'; exit 0 ;;\n            esac\n            sleep 2\n        done\n    else\n        while :; do\n            local act n\n            act=$(rpc aria2.tellActive)\n            n=$(printf '%s' \"$act\" | jq '.result | length')\n            printf '\\r\\033[K'\n            if [ \"$n\" -eq 0 ]; then\n                printf 'no active downloads\\n'\n                exit 0\n            fi\n            printf '%s' \"$act\" | jq -r \"$LIST_JQ\" | fmt_list | sed 's/^/  /' | paste -sd '\\n' -\n            # build multi-line? keep last row line only? \n```\nHmm — watch-all showing a full table every 2s with \\r only clears current line → messy. Better: for watch-all, show one line per active gid but clear all lines first. Simple approach: clear N lines via \\033[K per row after moving up. Complexity rising. Alternative for watch-all: print only the summary line: `N active — dl X/s up Y/s`, then per-gid single line each on separate rows with cursor-up clearing. Let me simplify: watch-all shows: line1 = summary (n active, total dl speed via getGlobalStat), line2.. = per-gid short rows, clearing by printing fixed height: use `\\033[<n>A` to move up n lines before rewrite:\n\n```bash\n            local lines=$((n + 1)) i\