in DID happen — producing one word — but bash -x RENDERS the argument as: `'\\nfunction f(s){ return sprintf(\"%d\", s) }\\n'` — and the `{ printf ... }` part is INSIDE the same quote but bash -x renders... no, the trace clearly has `' '{ printf` — quote, space, quote. The `' '` — hmm, actually is there a SPACE there? `' '{ printf` — single-quote, single-quote — that's the END of one arg `'` and START of next `'`? But `''` between them? The trace shows `'` (close), ` '` (space then open?) — \n\n`' '{ printf \"%s %s\\n\", $1, f(2) }'` — reading: `'` close arg2, ` ` space separator, `'` open arg3... so arg2=`\\nfunction f(s){ return sprintf(\"%d\", s) }\\n`, arg3=`{ printf \"%s %s\\n\", $1, f(2) }`. Two separate args confirmed.\n\nOK let me just run the definitive test with markers and also test whether the pipeline matters.\n\nActually — hold on, one more theory: the `.` — `(dirname)` — no. THE PIPE! `printf ... | awk ...` — the `\\` at end of the awk line... In a pipeline, bash reads the ENTIRE pipeline before executing. Line continuation would still apply...\n\nYou know what — maybe it's simpler than I think. Let me test the EXACT same thing WITHOUT the pipe:\n\n```bash\nawk -F'\\t' \"$PROG\"\\\n    '{ printf \"BODY:%s\\n\", f(2) }' <<< 'a'\n```\n\nand with pipe. And with echo. Let me just run all variants and compare outputs.",
