          "text": "Good question — let me answer the built-in first, then fold your idea into the plan.\n\n## Is there a built-in retry in aria2?\n\n**No long-term retry.** aria2 has `--max-tries` (default 5) + `--retry-wait` (default 0) — short, immediate retries *within* one attempt burst. When the network drops for a long time, aria2 burns through those tries in seconds, gives up, and moves the download to **STOPPED with status `error`**. Nothing waits an hour and retries. So your gap is real, and your idea (probe 8.8.8.8 to tell \"no internet\" from \"real problem\") is the right discriminator — aria2's error codes alone can't tell a dead source from a dead network.\n\n## Plan: `restart` + smart `retry`\n\n**`restart <gid>`** (already approved) — re-queue a stopped GID (truncated or full), torrents via rebuilt magnet, HTTP per-file with same dir/out. `--continue=true` means an errored partial file **resumes from where it stopped** — that's the \"and continue\" part.\n\n**`retry <gid|all> [--interval N] [--max-wait N] [--dir] [--seed] [--split] [--tmux]`** — built on restart, with your smart loop:\n\n1. **Probe** `net_up()`: `timeout 3 bash -c '</dev/tcp/8.8.8.8/53'` (TCP 53 to 8.8.8.8 — no ping dep, works even when ICMP is filtered; seam `NET_PROBE` env for tests).\n2. **Net down** → print `no internet — waiting for connectivity (Ctrl+C to abort)`, poll every `--interval` (default 30s) until it returns, then continue. `--max-wait 0` = wait forever (default), escape hatch `--max-wait <sec>` for scripts.\n3. **Net up** → `restart` the errored GID(s) (or `all` = every `status==\"error\"` in STOPPED), then poll the new GID a few seconds:\n   - starts downloading / completes instantly (file already on disk) → **success**, done.\n   - errors fast with `errorCode 3` (404/missing source) → **real issue**, print `errorMessage`, exit 1 — no blind re-loop.\n   - errors with network-ish code (timeout/1) → backoff-retry a few times, then give up with the diagnosis.\n\nBehavior on `retry all` with no internet: waits, then heals the whole errored queue when the network returns.\n\n### Changes\n- **`bin/pos-network-download`** — `resolve_gid`, `net_up`, `cmd_restart`, `cmd_retry` (+ poll helpers); `# POS_SUBCMDS:` → 21 (`restart`, `retry`); usage() + examples.\n- **Docs** — POS.md detail rows (restart, retry), howto/network.md (queue + outage-recovery recipe), AGENT_TODO; `make gen` regenerates tables/completions.\n- **Tests** — extend stub harness: restart HTTP/torrent/prefix, retry with `NET_PROBE=true` (up → restart fires) and `NET_PROBE=false` (down → waits, then retries when probe flips), real-404 diagnosis → rc=1, `retry all`, `--tmux`.\n- **Verify** — `make gen && make check` + suite green; commit `feat:`.\n\n### Questions before I implement\n1. **Both commands, or just `retry`?** I recommend both — `restart` is the instant \"back to history, download again\", `retry` is the smart resilience loop.\n2. **Should `watch <gid>` also auto-heal?** On `error`, probe: net down → show \"waiting for network\", auto-restart and keep watching when it returns; net up → report the real error. This makes your \"hour-long outage during a download\" story hands-off. Include it, or keep `watch` as-is and rely on `retry`?\n3. **Optional:** a systemd user timer that runs `retry all` every few minutes in the background — the queue heals itself with nobody watching. In scope, or later?",
