 (all ensure_daemon points + when queue becomes non-empty).\n  - Disable when: queue drains (no active, no waiting, no error in stopped). Check points: after any command that could drain the queue (watch exits, purge, remove, etc.)? That's a lot of touch points. Alternative: the timer service itself checks queue emptiness and disables itself when nothing to do. Cleaner! `pos network download retry all` → after doing its thing, if nothing active/waiting/errored → `systemctl --user disable --now pos-aria2-retry.timer`. That keeps all lifecycle logic in one place.\n  - And enable happens on add/restart/etc. Also `ensure_daemon` already runs for add/torrent/metalink — I can hook `ensure_timer` into those + restart/retry.\n\nBut careful: `retry all` being run *by* the timer must not try to disable the timer while the timer is still needed. The check is: after processing, if no active, no waiting, no error-status stopped → disable. If there are active downloads (still downloading) → keep enabled (they may fail later and need retry). If errored remain but net is down → keep enabled. Good.\n\nEdge: `watch <gid>` auto-heal — separate from timer; the timer also covers it as backup.\n\nAlso — \"retry work even just add\": another angle — maybe they also want that when add's download immediately errors (e.g., typo URL or net down), the user isn't left hanging. The timer covers net-down. For immediate real errors (404), the healer should diagnose: if after a few retries the error persists with errorCode 3, stop retrying that one (avoid infinite pointless loops) — my retry logic already has the real-issue detection. So `retry all` skips persistently-real-404 downloads (report once, don't keep re-adding). Need to make retry-all idempotent: don't re-retry a gid that was already retried and errored with a real code within the recent past. Hmm — simplest: `retry all` retries errored gids; if a retry produces a new errored gid with errorCode 3, mark the original as \"failed permanently\" (skip in future runs). Implement via a small state file? Or rely on aria2: the ORIGINAL errored gid stays in stopped with error; the retry creates a NEW gid that also errors with code 3. Next timer run would retry the NEW errored gid too → infinite. To avoid: only retry gids whose errorCode is network-ish (2 or 1)? But errorCode 1 is generic and could be a real issue too. The smart probe (net up + errorCode 3) → permanent fail. If net up + errorCode != 3 but errors immediately repeatedly → could also be permanent. \n\nBetter approach: `retry all` keeps a marker. For each errored gid it has already attempted to retry with net-up and the retried download failed within a grace window, it skips. Simplest robust: state file `~/.config/linux_post_install/download.retry` mapping gid → attempt count / last-attempt time. On retry attempt: if a previous attempt was within last 10 min and the new download errored with a real code (3) → stop retrying that gid (record it). Also cap attempts. Hmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify with a pragmatic rule:\n\n`retry all` logic:\n1. list stopped with status error.\n2. for each: if already retried (present in state with attempt>=3) → skip.\n3. net down → wait? NO — timer runs should NOT block waiting (they'd hold the service). For the TIMER invocation (`retry all --quiet --once`), no waiting: if net down → log \"net down, will retry next run\" and exit 0. The blocking-wait loop is only for the interactive `retry <gid>` / `watch`. Good — different modes:\n   - Interactive `retry <gid|all>` (user-invoked): blocking wait loop with messages.\n   - Timer `retry all --quiet`: one-shot, net-down → exit quietly, next run retries. This is cleaner.\n\nSo flags: `retry <gid|all> [--interval N] [--max-wait N] [--once] [--quiet]`. Default interactive: wait. `--once`: single pass (no wait loop). The timer uses `retry all --once --quiet`.\n\nState for permanent failures: keep a per-gid attempt counter in `$CONFIG_DIR/download.retry`. On net-up retry: increment; if new gid errors fast with errorCode 3 → mark permanent (won't retry again). This prevents infinite re-adding of dead URLs. Actually — maybe simpler: only mark permanent when errorCode==3 with net up. Otherwise keep retrying (networkish). The state file records gid → status (ok/permanent) + timestamp. Keep it minimal: file with one line per permanently-failed gid? But gids change on retry... The SOURCE identity: for HTTP, the URI is the identity; for torrent, the infohash. So mark permanent by URI/infohash. That survives gid changes. State file lines: `<uri-or-magnet-hash>`. On `retry all`, skip stopped errored gids whose primary uri/hash is in the permanent set. And `restart` (manual) always overrides (user explicitly asks) — maybe even clears the permanent marker? Manual restart of a permanently-failed gid: keep it manual, don't re-add to permanent set unless it fails again. Fine.\n\nHmm, is the state file overkill? The user explicitly wants smart distinction. I think permanent-failure memory is valuable but could be a v2. Let me propose it but flag it as a small state file; if they say too much, drop it. Actually — the retry loop already exits on real-issue diagnosis for interactive mode. For timer mode, the risk is re-adding a dead URL every 5 min forever. That's annoying but harmless-ish. Given \"keep it smart\", I'll include the permanent-marker file but keep it dead simple.\n\nNow systemd timer details (user units):\n- `pos-aria2-retry.service`:\n  ```\n  [Unit]\n  Description=pos aria2 retry healer (linux-post-install)\n  After=pos-aria2.service\n  [Service]\n  Type=oneshot\n  ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/pos network download retry all --once --quiet\n  ```\n  Wait — the tool path after install is `/usr/local/bin/pos-network-download` (or `pos`). postinstall copies bin/* → /usr/local/bin. So ExecStart should call the standalone tool: `/usr/local/bin/pos-network-download retry all --once --quiet`. But the installed path may vary; the tool 