# Threshold parse (renamed from eventer_parse_rule, operating on the RULE string)\n```bash\nsched_parse_rule() {  # $1 = \"op number[unit]\" e.g. \"> 60c\" — sets RULE_OP RULE_THRESH RULE_NUM\n    local line=\"$1\" s=\"\" m=\"\" parts=\"\" i\n    ...\n}\n```\nHmm — the old parser handles `\"msg\" if CMD OP THRESH` from a full line. Now MSG/CMD are separate keys and RULE is just `OP THRESH`. I can simplify: parse RULE by tokenizing and finding the rightmost op with a numeric RHS. Actually with RULE being exactly `OP THRESH`, simplest: \n```bash\nsched_parse_rule() {\n    local -a t=()\n    read -r -a t <<< \"$1\"\n    local n=${#t[@]}\n    [ \"$n\" -ge 2 ] || return 1\n    local i\n    for ((i=n-2; i>=0; i--)); do\n        case \"${t[i]}\" in\n            \">=\"|\"<=\"|\"==\"|\"!=\"|\">\"|\"<\")\n                if sched_numeric \"${t[i+1]}\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n                    RULE_OP=\"${t[i]}\"\n                    RULE_THRESH=\"${t[i+1]}\"\n                    RULE_NUM=\"$(sched_numeric \"${t[i+1]}\")\"\n                    return 0\n                fi ;;\n        esac\n    done\n    return 1\n}\n```\nBut wait — I want the RULE value to be usable in the job file as `RULE=\"> 60c\"`. When writing via config editor, we need to serialize `$RULE_OP $RULE_THRESH`. And migration: `RULE=\"<op> <thresh>\"`. Good.\n\nBut also — could RULE ever be a plain number with the op in the command? E.g. old eventer lines like `disk root > 80%` → RULE=\"> 80%\". And migration splits the old line. Also what about `== 1` (from event-rules.template: `smartctl -H /dev/sda | grep -q PASSED && echo 0 || echo 1 == 1`)? RULE=\"== 1\". OK.\n\nFor the check command first-numeric extraction, reuse:\n```bash\nsched_eval_check() {  # runs JOB_COMMAND → OUT_TEXT (full), OUT_RC, VALUE_NUM, VALUE_DISPLAY\n    ...\n}\n```\nThe old eventer_eval_check returned just first numeric of first line. For threshold I need the numeric; for other policies I need full output. So a unified runner:\n\n```bash\nsched_run_command() {  # sets JOB_OUT (full stdout+stderr), JOB_RC; returns JOB_RC\n    local out rc\n    if out=\"$(bash -c \"$JOB_COMMAND\" 2>&1)\"; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi\n    JOB_OUT=\"$out\"; JOB_RC=$rc\n    return 0\n}\n```\nHmm wait — `if out=\"$(...)\"; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi` — but `$?` inside the else branch: after the assignment fails, `$?` holds the command substitution's exit status. But careful — under `set -e`, the `if` condition doesn't trigger errexit. Good. But one subtlety: `bash -c \"$JOB_COMMAND\" 2>&1` — with command substitution `$(...)`, stderr redirection inside the subshell applies to the `bash -c` process. Actually `$(bash -c \"...\" 2>&1)` — the `2>&1` is inside the command substitution, applying to bash -c. OK.\n\nThen numeric extraction from JOB_OUT:\n```bash\nsched_first_numeric() {  # echo first numeric token across all lines of JOB_OUT; sets VALUE_DISPLAY\n    local tok line\n    while IFS= read -r line; do\n        read -r -a toks <<< \"$line\"\n        for tok in \"${toks[@]}\"; do\n            if sched_numeric \"$tok\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n                VALUE_NUM=\"$(sched_numeric \"$tok\")\"; VALUE_DISPLAY=\"$tok\"\n                return 0\n            fi\n        done\n    done <<< \"$JOB_OUT\"\n    return 1\n}\n```\nThe old eventer only checked the FIRST line for a number. Actually old: `line=\"$(printf '%s' \"$out\" | sed -n '1p')\"` then first numeric in that line. Hmm — for a check like `df -P / | awk 'NR==2{print $5+0}'` output is a single number on one line. For `smartctl`-style multi-line, threshold checks would want... keep parity with eventer (first line only)? The event-rules.template commands all output one line. Keeping first-line-only matches legacy exactly. But \"more flexible\" — a threshold check like `nproc` fine. I'll keep the eventer behavior (first line) to preserve migration semantics. Actually wait, old code: `out=\"$(bash -c \"$RULE_CMD\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"` then `line=\"$(printf '%s' \"$out\" | sed -n '1p')\"`. So yes, first line only, first numeric in it. Keep that.\n\nFor threshold display in list: same.\n\n### Notify message building\n\n```bash\nsched_msg_alert() {  # threshold crossed\n    if [ -n \"$JOB_MSG\" ]; then printf '%s (now %s)' \"$JOB_MSG\" \"$VALUE_DISPLAY\"\n    else printf '%s now %s is %s %s' \"${JOB_COMMAND%% *}\" \"$VALUE_DISPLAY\" \"$RULE_OP\" \"$RULE_THRESH\"; fi\n}\nsched_msg_recover() {\n    if [ -n \"$JOB_MSG\" ]; then printf '%s recovered (now %s)' \"$JOB_MSG\" \"$VALUE_DISPLAY\"\n    else printf '%s OK (now %s)' \"${JOB_COMMAND%% *}\" \"$VALUE_DISPLAY\"; fi\n}\n```\nWait — the auto-composed message used `${RULE_CMD%% *}` (first word of the command) — for JOB_COMMAND with a pipe the first word is e.g. `sudo`. That's what legacy did. Fine — keep.\n\nFor report policies (onchange/always/onerror): message = header + output:\n```bash\nsched_report_msg() {  # $1 = reason label\n    local label=\"$1\"\n    { \n        printf 'sched %s — %s\\n' \"$JOB_NAME\" \"$label\"\n        [ -z \"$JOB_MSG\" ] || printf '%s\\n' \"$JOB_MSG\"\n        [ -z \"$JOB_OUT\" ] || printf '%s\\n' \"$JOB_OUT\"\n    }\n}\n```\nHmm, ordering: header line then MSG then output. Or MSG as title. Let me do:\n```\nsched <name> [<MSG>] — <label>\n<output>\n```\nSimpler: \n```\nprintf 'sched %s (%s)%s\\n' \"$JOB_NAME\" \"$label\" \"${JOB_MSG:+ — $JOB_MSG}\"\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$JOB_OUT\"\n```\nTruncate to ~3800 chars (telegram listener precedent):\n```bash\nsched_truncate() {  # stdin → stdout capped at N chars\n    local n=\"${1:-3800}\" out\n    out=\"$(cat)\"\n    [ \"${#out}\" -gt \"$n\" ] && out=\"${out:0:$n}\"$'\\n…(truncated)'\n    printf '%s' \"$out\"\n}\n```\n\n### Policy runner (the core)\n\n```bash\nsched_run_job() {  # $1 = name; executes one job; notify per policy; returns 0\n    local name=\"$1\"\n    sched_read_job \"$name\" || { warn \"job '$name' unparseable — skipping\"; return 1; }\n    local out rc\n    sched_run_command   # sets JOB_OUT, JOB_RC\n    local policy=\"$JOB_NOTIFY\"\n    case \"$policy\" in\n        threshold) sched_policy_threshold ;;\n        onchange)  sched_policy_onchange ;;\n        always)    sched_policy_always ;;\n        onerror)   sched_policy_onerror ;;\n        never)     sched_policy_never ;;\n        *)         warn \"job '$name': unknown NOTIFY '$policy' (always|onchange|onerror|threshold|never)\"; sched_policy_never ;;\n    esac\n    sched_save_run \"$name\" \"$JOB_OUT\" \"$JOB_RC\"\n}\n```\n\nWait — save state: for onchange I need last output; for threshold I need firing flag; for all: last rc/time + log. Order: threshold uses JOB_OUT numeric; onchange compares JOB_OUT against stored `.out`. Save after policy decision? onchange needs the PREVIOUS output before overwriting `.out`. Let me structure:\n\n```bash\nsched_policy_threshold() {\n    if ! sched_first_numeric; then\n        warn \"job '$JOB_NAME' check produced no number — skipped\"\n        return 0\n    fi\n    local fired=0\n    sched_compare \"$VALUE_NUM\" \"$RULE_OP\" \"$RULE_NUM\" && fired=1\n    if [ \"$fired\" -eq 1 ] && ! sched_state_firing \"$JOB_NAME\"; then\n        notify_send \"$(sched_msg_alert)\"\n        sched_state_set_firing \"$JOB_NAME\" 1\n        log \"alert: $(sched_msg_alert)\"\n    elif [ \"$fired\" -eq 0 ] && sched_state_firing \"$JOB_NAME\"; then\n        notify_send \"$(sched_msg_recover)\"\n        sched_state_set_firing \"$JOB_NAME\" 0\n        log \"recovered: $(sched_msg_recover)\"\n    else\n        log \"ok: $JOB_NAME = $VALUE_DISPLAY (no change)\"\n    fi\n}\n```\n\nWait — state file for firing: per-job state file `$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/<name>.firing` containing 0/1? And `.out` for onchange + `.meta` for run record + `.log`? That's a few files per job: `<name>.firing`, `<name>.out`, `<name>.meta`, `<name>.log`. Cleaner: one state DIR per job? `$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/<name>/firing`, `<name>/out`, `<name>/meta`. That's tidy. Yes — per-job state dir.\n\nBut careful: legacy eventer state dir was `~/.local/share/linux_post_install/eventer/state/<hash>`. Migration doesn't carry state (rules reset) — acceptable, documented (\"editing a rule resets its state\" was already true).\n\n```bash\nsched_job_state_dir() { echo \"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$1\"; }\nsched_state_firing() { [ -f \"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$1/firing\" ] && grep -q '^1' \"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$1/firing\"; }\nsched_state_set_firing() { mkdir -p \"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$1\"; printf '%s\\n' \"$2\" > \"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$1/firing\"; }\n```\n\nonchange:\n```bash\nsched_policy_onchange() {\n    local dir=\"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$JOB_NAME\" prev=\"\"\n    if [ -f \"$dir/out\" ]; then\n        prev=\"$(cat \"$dir/out\")\"\n    fi\n    if [ -z \"$prev\" ] || [ \"$prev\" != \"$JOB_OUT\" ]; then\n        notify_send \"$(sched_report_msg 'changed' | sched_truncate)\"\n        log \"change detected — sent\"\n    else\n        log \"no change — not notifying\"\n    fi\n}\n```\nHmm, empty JOB_OUT: first run with empty output → prev=\"\" → treats as \"changed\" and notifies with empty-ish message. notify_send warns on empty message. For a command that outputs nothing: onchange would notify once on first run with a message that's just the header. Acceptable? For smartctl|grep with no matches, output empty — but rc=1, so onerror policy would be better. Actually for onchange with empty output → message = header only. Meh. But keep simple. Actually let me make onchange treat empty output as \"no change\" unless it differs... `[ \"$prev\" != \"$JOB_OUT\" ]` — prev=\"\" and JOB_OUT=\"\" → equal → \"no change, not notifying\". First run with empty output → prev=\"\" (no file) → we notify. Hmm, that notifies an empty-ish message once. For the smartctl example the output is never empty in practice. Keep: first run always notifies (documented), subsequent runs compare. Edge: first run with empty output notifies with header-only message — fine, informative (\"job produced no output\").\n\nWait — there's a subtle bug: `[ -f \"$dir/out\" ]` vs empty prev. If last output was empty (legitimately), the file exists but prev=\"\". Then `[ -z \"$prev\" ]` → treated as first-run → notify. That would re-notify an empty-output job every run (since it keeps writing empty out). Hmm. For the smartctl case output is never empty. But robustness: use file-existence rather than content-emptiness for \"first run\":\n```bash\nif [ -f \"$dir/out\" ]; then cmp -s \"$dir/out\" <(printf '%s' \"$JOB_OUT\") → same\n```\nUse a marker: compare `\"$dir/out\"` file with current via `diff -q` or sha. Cleaner:\n```bash\nlocal changed=0\nif [ ! -f \"$dir/out\" ]; then changed=1            # first run\nelif ! printf '%s' \"$JOB_OUT\" | cmp -s - \"$dir/out\"; then changed=1\nfi\n```\ncmp -s with stdin `-` vs file. \n\nalways: `notify_send \"$(sched_report_msg 'run' | sched_truncate)\"`.\nonerror:\n```bash\nsched_policy_onerror() {\n    if [ \"$JOB_RC\" -ne 0 ] || [ -z \"$JOB_OUT\" ]; then\n        notify_send \"$(sched_report_msg \"failed (rc $JOB_RC)\" | sched_truncate)\"\n        log \"error detected — sent\"\n    else\n        log \"ok (rc 0)\"\n    fi\n}\n```\nnever: log only.\n\n### Save run record + log\n```bash\nsched_save_run() {  # $1 name, $2 out, $3 rc\n    local dir=\"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$1\" logf=\"$SCHEDULE_LOG_DIR/$1.log\"\n    mkdir -p \"$dir\" \"$SCHEDULE_LOG_DIR\"\n    printf '%s' \"$2\" > \"$dir/out\"\n    printf 'rc=%s ts=%s\\n' \"$3\" \"$(date +%s)\" > \"$dir/meta\"\n    { printf '\\n== %s rc=%s ==\\n' \"$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')\" \"$3\"; printf '%s\\n' \"$2\"; } >> \"$logf\"\n    # rotate: keep last 500 lines\n    local n; n=\"$(wc -l < \"$logf\" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)\"\n    [ \"$n\" -gt 500 ] && tail -n 500 \"$logf\" > \"$logf.tmp\" && mv \"$logf.tmp\" \"$logf\"\n}\n```\nHmm — `tail > tmp && mv` under set -e is fine.\n\nWait — sched_save_run should be called AFTER the policy (so onchange compares before overwrite). Order in sched_run_job: run → policy (uses state) → save. But threshold/onchange policies read state; save writes `.out` + meta. For threshold we also keep `.firing` separate. Good.\n\nBut hold on — `never` policy + save: fine.\n\nAlso `--dry-run` for `run`? eventer's run supports --dry-run (prints \"would fire\"). For scheduler: `pos system schedule run <name> --dry-run` → run the command, print what would be sent WITHOUT sending. notify_send isn't dry-run aware... I'll gate: if DRY_RUN, log \"(dry-run) would notify: <msg>\" instead of notify_send. For never jobs nothing anyway. Implement via a wrapper in the tool:\n```bash\nsched_notify() {\n    if [ \"${DRY_RUN:-0}\" -eq 1 ]; then log \"(dry-run) would notify: $1\"; else notify_send \"$1\"; fi\n}\n```\nAnd use sched_notify everywhere in policies instead of notify_send directly. Good — keeps lib DRY_RUN-aware.\n\n### Timers\n\n```bash\nsched_unit() { echo \"${SCHED_PREFIX}-$1\"; }\n\nsched_write_units() {  # $1 name $2 oncal\n    local name=\"$1\" oncal=\"$2\" base=\"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR/$(sched_unit \"$name\")\"\n    mkdir -p \"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR\"\n    cat > \"$base.service\" <<EOF\n[Unit]\nDescription=pos system schedule — run job $name\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nExecStart=$SCHED_RUNNER run $name\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=timers.target\nEOF\n    cat > \"$base.timer\" <<EOF\n[Unit]\nDescription=Schedule: pos system schedule — $name\n\n[Timer]\nOnCalendar=$oncal\nPersistent=true\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=timers.target\nEOF\n    chmod 644 \"$base.service\" \"$base.timer\"\n}\n```\n\n`interval_to_oncalendar` + `interval_label` — copy from eventer (they exist there).\n\n`ensure_linger` — copy from eventer.\n\nSync/reconcile:\n```bash\nsched_sync() {\n    local name oncal n=0 wrote=0\n    local -a jobs=()\n    mapfile -t jobs < <(sched_list_jobs)\n    [ \"${#jobs[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && log \"no jobs in $SCHEDULE_DIR — add one with 'pos system schedule config'\"\n    for name in \"${jobs[@]}\"; do\n        if ! sched_read_job \"$name\"; then warn \"job '$name' unparseable — skipping\"; continue; fi\n        [ \"$JOB_ENABLED\" = \"false\" ] && continue\n        if ! oncal=\"$(interval_to_oncalendar \"$JOB_INTERVAL\")\"; then\n            warn \"invalid interval '$JOB_INTERVAL' for '$name' — skipping\"\n            continue\n        fi\n        if [ \"${DRY_RUN:-0}\" -eq 1 ]; then\n            log \"(dry-run) write $(sched_unit \"$name\").timer (OnCalendar=$oncal)\"\n        else\n            sched_write_units \"$name\" \"$oncal\"\n        fi\n        wrote=1; n=$((n+1))\n    done\n    if [ \"${DRY_RUN:-0}\" -ne 1 ]; then\n        [ \"$wrote\" -eq 1 ] && systemctl --user daemon-reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n        [ \"$n\" -gt 0 ] && ensure_linger\n        for name in \"${jobs[@]}\"; do\n            if ! sched_read_job \"$name\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || [ \"$JOB_ENABLED\" = \"false\" ]; then continue; fi\n            interval_to_oncalendar \"$JOB_INTERVAL\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || continue\n            local t=\"$(sched_unit \"$name\").timer\"\n            if systemctl --user is-enabled \"$t\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n                systemctl --user restart \"$t\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n            else\n                systemctl --user enable --now \"$t\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || warn \"could not enable timer '$t' (is the user systemd manager running?)\"\n            fi\n        done\n        sched_remove_orphans\n        sched_cleanup_legacy\n    fi\n}\n```\nHmm — this is getting long. `sched_remove_orphans`: remove `pos-schedule-*.timer/.service` for jobs not in schedule.d (or disabled). And `sched_cleanup_legacy`: remove old `pos-event-trigger.{service,timer}` + daemon-reload.\n\nWait, re-read: with ENABLED=false jobs — the units for disabled jobs should be REMOVED by orphans logic (their name is in schedule.d but disabled → treat as orphan). So orphan = unit whose name is not a valid ENABLED job.\n\n```bash\nsched_remove_orphans() {\n    local f p removed=0\n    for f in \"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR\"/${SCHED_PREFIX}-*.timer; do\n        [ -f \"$f\" ] || continue\n        p=\"${f##*/}\"; p=\"${p#${SCHED_PREFIX}-}\"; p=\"${p%.timer}\"\n        if ! sched_read_job \"$p\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || [ \"$JOB_ENABLED\" = \"false\" ]; then\n            systemctl --user disable --now \"$(sched_unit \"$p\").timer\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n            rm -f \"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR/$(sched_unit \"$p\").timer\" \"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR/$(sched_unit \"$p\").service\"\n            log \"removed timer for '$p'\"\n            removed=1\n        fi\n    done\n    [ \"$removed\" -eq 1 ] && systemctl --user daemon-reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n}\n```\n\nLegacy cleanup:\n```bash\nsched_cleanup_legacy() {\n    local f=\"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR/${SCHED_LEGACY_UNIT}.timer\"\n    if [ -f \"$f\" ]; then\n        systemctl --user disable --now \"$SCHED_LEGACY_UNIT.timer\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n        rm -f \"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR/${SCHED_LEGACY_UNIT}.timer\" \"$USER_SYSTEMD_DIR/${SCHED_LEGACY_UNIT}.service\"\n        systemctl --user daemon-reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n        log \"removed legacy ${SCHED_LEGACY_UNIT} timer (superseded by per-job timers)\"\n    fi\n}\n```\n\nEnable/disable:\n```bash\nsched_enable() {  # $1 = name|all\n    if [ \"$1\" = \"all\" ]; then sched_sync; return; fi\n    # enable specific job: clear ENABLED=false\n    ...\n}\nsched_disable() {  # $1 = name|all\n    ...\n}\n```\nFor per-job: write `ENABLED=false` into job file (or clear). `enable <name>` removes the ENABLED=false line; `disable <name>` sets it. Then sched_sync (or targeted sync). Write via grep -v + append:\n\n```bash\nsched_set_enabled() {  # $1 name, $2 true|false\n    local f=\"$(sched_job_file \"$1\")\" tmp\n    [ -f \"$f\" ] || err \"no such job '$1'\"\n    if [ \"${DRY_RUN:-0}\" -eq 1 ]; then log \"(dry-run) set ENABLED=$2 in $f\"; return; fi\n    tmp=\"$(mktemp)\"\n    grep -v '^ENABLED=' \"$f\" > \"$tmp\" || true\n    printf 'ENABLED=%s\\n' \"$2\" >> \"$tmp\"\n    mv \"$tmp\" \"$f\"\n    chmod 600 \"$f\"\n}\n```\nThen `enable <name>`: sched_set_enabled name true; sched_sync. `disable <name>`: set false; sched_sync. `enable` (no arg = all): sched_sync (enables everything not disabled). `disable` no arg: disable ALL — set ENABLED=false for every job? That edits all files... maybe simpler: `disable` without name errors with hint, or disables all jobs. Old event-trigger `disable` removed the whole timer. New equivalent: disable all jobs. I'll do: `disable` (no arg) → sets ENABLED=false on every job + sync (removes all timers). `enable` (no arg) → sync (re-enables per-file state). Hmm, that asymmetry... `enable` no-arg can't know which to enable — it enables all (clears ENABLED=false from all). OK symmetric:\n- `enable` → clear ENABLED=false on all jobs + sync\n- `disable` → set ENABLED=false on all jobs + sync\n- `enable <name>` / `disable <name>` → per job + sync.\n\nHmm wait, `enable` clearing all ENABLED=false — maybe user disabled one intentionally then runs `enable` → everything back on. That matches \"enable = turn everything on\". Fine, documented.\n\nActually — hold on. Do we even need ENABLED at all? Alternative minimal model: jobs are enabled by presence; `disable <name>` REMOVES the timer via sync without touching the file? Then \"disabled\" isn't persistent... sync re-enables it next time. So persistence needs the marker. Keep ENABLED key. Fine — it's one line in the file, and `list` shows enabled/disabled.\n\nActually wait, simpler alternative: skip ENABLED entirely for v1 — `disable <name>` deletes the job's timer units and marks a separate `.disabled` file? No, same complexity. Or: `disable` = `sched_remove` (rm the job)? No — user wants to stop temporarily. Keep ENABLED.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — minimalism. The core ask: scheduler with per-job intervals + notify policies. enable/disable per job is nice-to-have. Old CLI had enable/disable/status. Keep them with ENABLED key — it's ~15 lines total. OK.\n\n### status\n\n```bash\nsched_status() {\n    local jobs=() name\n    mapfile -t jobs < <(sched_list_jobs)\n    [ \"${#jobs[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && { echo \"no jobs defined in $SCHEDULE_DIR\"; echo \"add one: pos system schedule config\"; return; }\n    if ! systemctl --user show-environment >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n        warn \"no user systemd manager reachable — timers can't run\"\n    fi\n    for name in \"${jobs[@]}\"; do\n        if ! sched_read_job \"$name\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo \"  $name  INVALID job file\"; continue; fi\n        local st=\"enabled\"; [ \"$JOB_ENABLED\" = \"false\" ] && st=\"disabled\"\n        local oncal next=\"\"\n        oncal=\"$(interval_to_oncalendar \"$JOB_INTERVAL\" 2>/dev/null || echo \"$JOB_INTERVAL\")\"\n        if [ \"$st\" = \"enabled\" ] && systemctl --user show-environment >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n            next=\"$(systemctl --user list-timers \"$(sched_unit \"$name\").timer\" --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1, $2}' | head -1)\"\n        fi\n        printf '  %-18s %-8s %-9s %s%s\\n' \"$name\" \"$JOB_NOTIFY\" \"$st\" \"$oncal${next:+ (next: $next)}\"\n    done\n}\n```\nHmm — awk on list-timers output: `systemctl list-timers` columns: NEXT LEFT LAST PASSED UNIT ACTIVATES. First two fields = NEXT date, LEFT. eventer used `awk '{print $1, $2}'`. OK.\n\n### list\n```bash\nsched_list() {\n    local jobs=() name\n    mapfile -t jobs < <(sched_list_jobs)\n    if [ \"${#jobs[@]}\" -eq 0 ]; then\n        echo \"(no jobs — run 'pos system schedule config' to add one)\"\n        return\n    fi\n    printf '%-18s %-9s %-9s %-10s %s\\n' JOB NOTIFY INTERVAL STATUS LAST\n    for name in \"${jobs[@]}\"; do\n        if ! sched_read_job \"$name\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n            printf '%-18s %s\\n' \"$name\" \"INVALID job file\"\n            continue\n        fi\n        local st=\"enabled\"; [ \"$JOB_ENABLED\" = \"false\" ] && st=\"disabled\"\n        local last=\"never\"\n        if [ -f \"$SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR/$name/meta\" ]; then\n            last=\"$(sched_meta_str \"$name\")\"\n        fi\n        printf '%-18s %