ched_sync? Hmm — editor runs under user's shell; sync requires systemd. Eventer didn't sync on edit. Keep: no auto-sync in editor; hint \"run 'pos system schedule enable' to apply\". Good — least surprise.\n\n- `edit <n>`: re-prompt each field (blank keeps). Command re-entry must be literal. Prompt: \"command (blank keeps): \".\n- `remove <n>`: confirm, rm file (+ state dir? leave state).\n- `enable <n>`/`disable <n>`: toggle ENABLED in file.\n\nValidation for interval: `interval_to_oncalendar \"$v\" >/dev/null || warn`. For notify: case match; threshold requires RULE.\n\nWriting job files:\n```bash\nsched_write_job() {  # $1 name — writes JOB_* globals to file\n    local f=\"$(sched_job_file \"$1\")\" tmp\n    mkdir -p \"$SCHEDULE_DIR\"\n    tmp=\"$(mktemp)\"\n    {\n        printf '# pos system schedule job — %s\\n' \"$1\"\n        [ -z \"$JOB_INTERVAL\" ] || printf 'INTERVAL=%s\\n' \"$JOB_INTERVAL\"\n        printf 'NOTIFY=%s\\n' \"$JOB_NOTIFY\"\n        [ -z \"$JOB_MSG\" ] || printf 'MSG=%s\\n' \"$JOB_MSG\"\n        [ -z \"$JOB_RULE\" ] || printf 'RULE=%s\\n' \"$JOB_RULE\"\n        printf 'COMMAND=%s\\n' \"$JOB_COMMAND\"\n    } > \"$tmp\"\n    mv \"$tmp\" \"$f\"\n    chmod 600 \"$f\"\n}\n```\nValues with spaces: INTERVAL=5m no spaces; NOTIFY no spaces; MSG could have spaces → unquoted line `MSG=CPU too hot` — parse takes remainder after `MSG=` and strips quotes (if quoted). If unquoted with spaces, strip_quotes leaves as-is → \"CPU too hot\". Fine. Writing unquoted is fine since we parse remainder. But careful: writing `MSG=CPU too hot` then read: `v=\"${line#*=}\"` → \"CPU too hot\" → strip quotes (none) → fine.\n\nFor ENABLED toggling — sched_set_enabled handles.\n\n### migrate\n\n```bash\nsched_migrate() {\n    local legacy=\"$SCHED_LEGACY_ENV\"\n    if [ ! -f \"$legacy\" ]; then\n        log \"no legacy event.env found at $legacy — nothing to migrate\"\n        return\n    fi\n    local line n=0 i=0 ok migrated=0\n    local -a LEGACY_RULES=()\n    while IFS=