            "output": "<path>/mnt/hdd/ciya_space/work/repo/Linux_post_install/DOC/DEV.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Development Guide\n2: \n3: How this repo works, how to add features, and what to keep in mind when editing.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## Architecture\n8: \n9: ### Installation Phases\n10: \n11: ```\n12:                   install.sh\n13:                       │\n14:           ┌───────────┼───────────┐\n15:           ▼           ▼           ▼\n16:     preinstall.sh   bin/*    postinstall.sh\n17:     (packages)   → /usr/local/bin  (config + services)\n18: ```\n19: \n20: | Phase | Script | Responsibility |\n21: |-------|--------|----------------|\n22: | Pre | `preinstall.sh` | System packages, apt repos, global binaries (yt-dlp) |\n23: | Install | `install.sh` | Copies `bin/*` → `/usr/local/bin/` (chmod 755), `lib/common.sh` + `lib/flags.sh` + `lib/entertainment-lib.sh` → `/usr/local/bin/` (chmod 644) |\n24: | Post | `postinstall.sh` | User config (SSH keys, PATH, bash completion), systemd services |\n25: \n26: Each phase is independent and runs only if the corresponding script exists.\n27: \n28: ### Directory Layout\n29: \n30: | Directory | Purpose | Installed To |\n31: |-----------|---------|-------------|\n32: | `bin/` | Daily-use CLI tools and wrappers | `/usr/local/bin/` |\n33: | `apps/<category>/` | Optional desktop app installers | run on demand |\n34: | `lib/` | Shared libraries: `common.sh` (helpers), `flags.sh` (feature flags), `notify.sh` (multi-platform alerting), `entertainment-lib.sh` (entertainment scheduling) | sourced at build time |\n35: | `config/` | Gitignored user config files | `~/.config/<app>/` (via postinstall) |\n36: | `entertainment/` | Public-API plugins for the entertainment module | `/usr/local/bin` (via install.sh Phase 2) |\n37: | `compose/` | ScaleTail templates (git submodule) | `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail` |\n38: | `systemd/` | Systemd unit files | `/etc/systemd/system/` (via postinstall) |\n39: \n40: ### The `pos` CLI\n41: \n42: `bin/pos` is a smart dispatcher. It scans its own directory for executable `pos-*` files and uses variable-length argument matching:\n43: \n44: ```\n45: pos docker compose up jellyfin\n46:   → tries pos-docker-compose-up-jellyfin  (not found)\n47:   → tries pos-docker-compose-up           (not found)\n48:   → finds pos-docker-compose              → runs with args \"up jellyfin\"\n49: ```\n50: \n51: All non-interactive commands log to `~/.local/share/linux_post_install/logs/`.\n52: \n53: `pos help <full command>` shows a tool's help, e.g. `pos help communication telegram-sender` (all words joined with dashes → `pos-communication-telegram-sender --help`). `pos <category>` or `pos <category> --help` shows a category's subcommands (derived from the `pos-<category>-*` filenames in `bin/` — no script execution, so it works even for root-only/interactive tools like `system-firewall`).\n54: \n55: **When adding a command, `bin/pos` itself has one thing to keep in sync:**\n56: \n57: - **The usage text** (`usage()` function) — the CATEGORIES block is **auto-derived** from the `pos-*` filenames in `bin/` (no manual edit, can't drift). The EXAMPLES block is the only hand-maintained part: add a line there only if you want the tool showcased in `pos --help`.\n58: - **`INTERACTIVE_CMDS`** (space-separated list above the dispatch loop) — commands that **read stdin** (password prompts, selection menus: `system-firewall`, `media-mp4`, `system-backup`, `share-usb-server`, `communication-telegram-listener`) must be added here. Everything else is piped through `tee` for logging, which would hang or swallow an interactive prompt. sudo's own password prompt is unaffected — it reads from `/dev/tty`. Trade-off: it's all-or-nothing **per script** — adding a flag-style tool with *any* prompting subcommand (e.g. `share-usb-server --share`) means *every* subcommand of that script skips output logging (e.g. `pos share usb server --ls` loses the `tee` log too).\n59: \n60: ### Shared Library (`lib/common.sh`)\n61: \n62: Sourced by most scripts. Key functions:\n63: \n64: | Function | Purpose |\n65: |----------|---------|\n66: | `log \"msg\"` | Green `[+]` status message |\n67: | `warn \"msg\"` | Yellow `[!]` warning |\n68: | `err \"msg\"` | Red `ERROR:` + exit 1 |\n69: | `ok \"msg\"` | Green `OK` prefix |\n70: | `section \"title\"` | Cyan-bordered section header |\n71: | `step N T \"msg\"` | Numbered step header |\n72: | `run cmd` | Executes command, respects `$DRY_RUN` |\n73: | `spawn \"msg\" cmd` | Animated braille spinner + elapsed time |\n74: | `timer_start` / `timer_stop` | Elapsed time tracking |\n75: | `confirm \"prompt\"` | y/N prompt with optional default |\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## Adding a New CLI Tool\n80: \n81: Start from the template: `cp templates/pos-tool.sh bin/pos-<category>-<command> && chmod +x bin/pos-<category>-<command>`.\n82: \n83: ### 1. Create the script\n84: \n85: ```bash\n86: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n87: \n88: set -euo pipefail\n89: \n90: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/common.sh\"\n91: \n92: usage() {\n93:     cat <<EOF\n94: Usage: my-tool <argument>\n95: EOF\n96:     exit 0\n97: }\n98: \n99: case \"${1:-}\" in\n100:     -h|--help|\"\") usage ;;\n101: esac\n102: \n103: # --- script logic ---\n104: ```\n105: \n106: **Conventions:**\n107: - Shebang: `#!/usr/bin/env bash`\n108: - Strict mode: `set -euo pipefail`\n109: - `--help` flag: accept `-h` / `--help` via `case` pattern\n110: - **Deps guards run before `--help`:** `command -v <bin> &>/dev/null || err \"… (install <pkg>)\"` lines sit at the top of the script, **before** the `-h|--help` case — so `--help` also errors when a dependency is missing. This matches every existing deps-gated tool; keep it that way.\n111: - Shared library: always source `common.sh` for colors, logging, spinners\n112: - Exit codes: `0` success, `1` error\n113: - No shared lib? Inline fallbacks:\n114:   ```bash\n115:   log()  { echo \"[+] $*\"; }\n116:   warn() { echo \"[!] $*\"; }\n117:   err()  { echo \"ERROR: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n118:   ```\n119:   If you skip `common.sh`, `make gen` adds the tool to the \"Scripts that do NOT source common.sh\" list in `DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md` automatically.\n120: \n121: ### 2. Make it discoverable\n122: \n123: - The dispatcher auto-discovers executable `bin/pos-*` files — no registration needed. The file **must be executable** (`chmod +x`, committed as mode `100755`); the dispatcher and `install.sh` skip non-executables.\n124: - `pos <category> --help` (and bare `pos <category>`) is derived from the `pos-<category>-*` filenames too — a new tool appears in its category's help automatically, with no registration (see [The `pos` CLI](#the-pos-cli)).\n125: - **Add the `# POS:` header** (single source of truth for the docs) right after the shebang/strict-mode lines:\n126:   ```bash\n127:   # POS: <category> <command> — one-line description rendered by `make gen`\n128:   # POS_FLAGS: --flag1 --flag2      # ONLY for flag-style tools\n129:   # POS_SUBCMDS: sub1 sub2          # ONLY for multi-command tools\n130:   ```\n131:   The description feeds the dispatch table, bin tree and file table in `DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md`; `POS_FLAGS` feeds flag completion and `POS_SUBCMDS` feeds subcommand completion in `completions/pos.bash` (both update via `make gen`). `make gen` only reads the text after the first `— ` — the `<category> <command>` words before it are convention-only (for nested tools, keep the full path there, e.g. `# POS: communication telegram-listener — …`).\n132: - **Category-less vs categorized:** most tools are `bin/pos-<category>-<command>`. Use category-less `bin/pos-<cmd>` (e.g. `pos-config`, `pos-tree`) only for dispatcher/dev-level commands that fit no category — they dispatch and document like any tool but show with an empty category in the generated tables.\n133: - Nested tools (e.g. `bin/pos-communication-telegram-listener`) are auto-detected from filenames: the trailing segment (`listener`) is offered as a subcommand of the parent tool (`communication-telegram`) in `pos <category> --help` and tab-completion, instead of appearing as a flat sibling (`telegram-listener`). The flat dash-form (`pos communication telegram-listener`) still dispatches.\n134: - Optionally add an EXAMPLES line in `bin/pos` `usage()` to showcase the tool in `pos --help`.\n135: - If the command **reads stdin** (prompts/selection), add it to `INTERACTIVE_CMDS` in `bin/pos` — see [The `pos` CLI](#the-pos-cli).\n136: \n137: ### 3. Add system dependencies\n138: \n139: Add package names to the `PACKAGES` array in `preinstall.sh`:\n140: \n141: ```bash\n142: PACKAGES=(\n143:     ...\n144:     your-package\n145: )\n146: ```\n147: \n148: **Not in apt?** If the dependency ships as a manual installer (no package — e.g. `usbsrv`, the USB Redirector server), do **not** put it in `PACKAGES` (that would break `preinstall.sh`). Instead, add a `command -v <binary> || err \"… install from <URL>\"` guard in the tool itself and note the manual install in `usage()`/`DOC/POS.md`.\n149: \n150: ### 4. Config files (if needed)\n151: \n152: Two kinds of config, don't mix them up:\n153: \n154: - **Machine defaults shipped by the installer:** place the file in `config/` and add copy logic to `postinstall.sh`. If it contains secrets, add to `.gitignore` and document in `DOC/`.\n155: - **Runtime tool config set by the user:** `~/.config/linux_post_install/<tool>.env` with `chmod 600`. Load it with env-var precedence (flags > environment > file). Patterns: `pos-docker-compose` (`compose.env`), `pos-communication-telegram-sender` (`telegram.env`, edited via `pos config telegram` — token masked), and the shared ones below. Never store tokens in the repo.\n156:   - `system.env` — shared \"system\" settings loaded by `pos-system-*` tools via `load_system_env()` in `lib/common.sh` (currently `BACKUP_SERVICE_ROOTS`, `HEALTH_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_DAYS`). Env already exported wins over the file.\n157:   - `notify.env` — alerting platform selection (`NOTIFY_PLATFORM=telegram,matrix`), read by `lib/notify.sh`.\n158: \n159: ### 5. Add SSH keys (if needed)\n160: \n161: Place public keys in `config/authorized_keys` (one per line). `postinstall.sh` reads this file automatically.\n162: \n163: ### 6. Update the docs\n164: \n165: - `DOC/POS.md`: add the command to the section table + a detail block (commands, behavior, configuration). This is the one hand-written doc.\n166: - `DOC/HOWTO.md` index row + a hands-on section in `DOC/howto/<category>.md` (recipes + troubleshooting) for user-facing tools.\n167: - `DOC/AGENT_Context_Project.md` generated sections (bin tree, dispatch table, no-common.sh list, line-count table) and the `completions/pos.bash` flags block are produced by `make gen` — do **not** hand-edit between the `GEN:START`/`GEN:END` markers. Hand-maintained, not gen-checked: the line-count rows **above** the filetable marker (non-`pos-*` files only — bump a row's count when that file's length changes) and the \"Common Tasks for Agents\" table (add a row for the new tool).\n168: - `AGENTS.md` Quick facts: update if a structural fact changed (new category, new convention).\n169: - Root `README.md`: only if the `pos` category list in the help text changes.\n170: - Move the finished task to the `Done` section of `AGENT_TODO.md` (dated) in the same commit.\n171: \n172: ### 7. Test\n173: \n174: ```bash\n175: chmod +x bin/your-tool\n176: bash -n bin/your-tool\n177: shellcheck bin/your-tool\n178: ./bin/your-tool --help\n179: env-seam review: every path the tool writes must be `VAR=\"${VAR:-default}\"`-guarded —\n180:   grep for config writes without a `:-` guard: `grep -nE '>\\s*(\\$HOME|/etc)' bin/your-tool lib/your-lib`\n181:   (each hit needs the seam; prove it with `VAR=/tmp/x bin/your-tool …` + assert the real path is untouched)\n182: bin/pos help <full command>   # confirm dispatch works\n183: bin/pos <category> --help     # confirm category listing includes the new tool (first tool in a new category)\n184: make gen                      # regenerate doc tables + completion flags\n185: make check                    # full self-consistency gate (syntax, exec bits, doc/code sync, smoke)\n186: ```\n187: \n188: `make check` is the definition of done — the same check runs as a pre-commit hook once you've run `make hook`.\n189: \n190: ### Testing tools that need root / systemd / missing deps\n191: \n192: `make check` only proves syntax, exec bits, doc sync and dispatch — not behaviour. For tools that need `sudo`, systemd, or binaries absent from the dev box (samba, usbsrv, …), test them end-to-end with two patterns:\n193: \n194: - **Env-overridable paths.** Anything that touches a system config location gets an env override whose default is the real path — the seam that lets the tool be exercised against temp files. Precedents: `FLAGS_DIR` (`lib/flags.sh`), `SMB_CONF` (`bin/pos-share-smb-server`, default `/etc/samba/smb.conf`), `SMB_CREDS_DIR`/`UNIT_DIR` (`bin/pos-share-smb-client`), `USER_SYSTEMD_DIR` (`bin/pos-network-download`, `bin/pos-communication-{telegram,matrix}-listener`, `lib/scheduler-lib.sh` — write it as `${USER_SYSTEMD_DIR:-${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user}`), and the scheduler's `SCHEDULE_DIR`/`SCHEDULE_STATE_DIR`/`SCHEDULE_LOG_DIR`/`SCHED_LEGACY_ENV` (`lib/scheduler-lib.sh`). Pick a short tool-specific name and don't advertise it in `usage()` — it's a test seam, not user-facing. **Gotcha (session-learned):** a `VAR=\"${XDG…:-…}\"` without the leading `VAR:-` *overrides* the seam — the stub run then silently writes to the real `$HOME` path and every assertion passes while the bug hides. The override must be written first, then tested with `VAR=/tmp/x …` and a check that the real path is untouched.\n195: - **Stub PATH.** Create a temp dir with fake binaries, then run the tool with `PATH=\"$stubs:$PATH\"`: fake `sudo` → `exec \"$@\"`; fake `systemctl`/`smbcontrol`/`mount.cifs` → echo their args; fake `testparm` → `cat` the file back (so validation passes); fake `systemd-escape` → print a fixed name. Assert on output **and** exit codes — happy path plus each failure path (`err` sets rc=1).\n196: - **Interactive prompts** (`read … </dev/tty`): drive them with a PTY — `printf 'answer\\n' | script -qec \"cmd\" /dev/null` — then assert the side effect (e.g. the chmod-600 creds file lands with the right mode).\n197: \n198: Example (session-learned): `PATH=/tmp/stubs:$PATH SMB_CONF=/tmp/smb.conf bin/pos-share-smb-server share /tmp/media …`.\n199: \n200: Stub harnesses are **throwaway by design**: no `tests/` dir and no CI in this repo — build them outside the project (`/tmp/opencode/<tool>-test/`: `stubs/` + `run-tests.sh` with a `check \"desc\" \"expected\" \"$actual\"` helper and a pass/fail count), run them, then leave them in `/tmp`. Only the *pattern* above is worth keeping in the repo.\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## Adding an Entertainment Plugin\n205: \n206: The `entertainment` module routes public-API data to Telegram via the single runner `pos entertainment send <plugin>` (`bin/pos-entertainment-send`). Auto-triggering is config-driven: `ENABLED` in `entertainment.env` holds `plugin, interval` pairs; the tools `pos entertainment config|enable|disable|status` (`bin/pos-entertainment-*`) reconcile the schedule. All shared logic (ENABLED parsing, interval→schedule mapping, scheduler sync) lives in `lib/entertainment-lib.sh` — sourced by the `pos-entertainment-*` tools (never by plugins). The scheduler is **systemd user timers** — the only backend (requires a reachable user systemd manager).\n207: \n208: ### 1. Create the plugin\n209: \n210: Drop an executable script in `entertainment/<name>.sh` with a `# POS_PLUGIN: <name>` marker on line 3 (this is what makes it a plugin — the installed runner lists plugins by this marker, not by `.sh` files, since `/usr/local/bin` is shared with other tooling). Declare every config key the plugin reads with `# POS_KEYS: <KEY> <description> (required|optional)` lines right after it — `pos entertainment config` prints these in its Keys section and uses them to warn/not-warn on `config set`:\n211: \n212: ```bash\n213: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n214: set -euo pipefail\n215: # POS_PLUGIN: myplugin\n216: # POS_KEYS: MYPLUGIN_URL <feed url> (required)\n217: # POS_KEYS: MYPLUGIN_TAG <filter tag> (optional)\n218: err() { echo \"ERROR: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n219: \n220: command -v curl &>/dev/null || err \"curl not found\"\n221: \n222: data=\"$(curl -fsS --max-time 20 https://api.example.com/foo)\"\n223: printf 'Title: %s\\n' \"$data\"\n224: ```\n225: \n226: **Contract:** plugins are **self-contained** — do **not** source `lib/common.sh`. Its `log`/`warn`/`ok` helpers print to **stdout**, and the runner captures stdout as the message to send (helper chatter would be sent to Telegram). All stdout is the message; errors go to stderr and exit nonzero. Plugins must be non-interactive (no prompts) — the module is designed for systemd user timers.\n227: \n228: ### 2. Config (if needed)\n229: \n230: Read runtime values from `~/.config/linux_post_install/entertainment.env` (chmod 600, env precedence) — same pattern as `telegram.env`. Example: `weather.sh` uses `WEATHER_LAT`/`WEATHER_LON`. Declare each key with a `# POS_KEYS:` header line (see step 1) so `pos entertainment config` lists it and `config set` recognizes it.\n231: \n232: ### 3. Deps\n233: \n234: `curl` and `jq` are already in `preinstall.sh` PACKAGES. Anything else: guard with `command -v … || err \"…\"` and, if apt-available, add to PACKAGES.\n235: \n236: ### 4. Done\n237: \n238: Plugins are not `pos-*` tools, so `make gen`/`make check` don't scan them — verify with `bash -n entertainment/<name>.sh` and a live `pos entertainment send <name> --print` run. Document the plugin in `DOC/POS.md`'s entertainment plugin table.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## Adding an Optional App\n243: \n244: Start from the template: `cp templates/app.sh apps/<category>/<name>.sh`.\n245: \n246: ### 1. Create the installer\n247: \n248: ```bash\n249: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n250: set -euo pipefail\n251: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../../lib/common.sh\"\n252: \n253: install_myapp() {\n254:     command -v myapp &>/dev/null && { log \"myapp already installed\"; return 0; }\n255:     spawn \"Installing myapp\" sudo apt install -y myapp\n256: }\n257: \n258: uninstall_myapp() {\n259:     command -v myapp &>/dev/null || { log \"myapp not installed\"; return 0; }\n260:     spawn \"Removing myapp\" sudo apt purge -y myapp\n261:     spawn \"Cleaning up dependencies\" sudo apt autoremove -y\n262: }\n263: \n264: case \"${1:-}\" in\n265:     uninstall) uninstall_myapp ;;\n266:     *) install_myapp ;;\n267: esac\n268: ```\n269: \n270: Place it in `apps/<category>/<name>.sh`. It auto-appears in the picker — no registration needed.\n271: \n272: **Categories:** `browsers`, `development`, `media`, `networking`, `remote-access`, `system`, `utilities`\n273: \n274: **Docs:** add a row to the catalog table in `DOC/APPS.md` (name, category, purpose, install method).\n275: \n276: ### 2. Conventions\n277: \n278: - Idempotent: check `command -v` (or `flatpak list` / file existence) before installing **and** uninstalling\n279: - Every app **must** provide an `uninstall_<name>()` function and dispatch on `uninstall` via the `case` above — `apps/install.sh --uninstall` depends on it\n280: - APT packages → `sudo apt install -y` inside `spawn`, remove with `sudo apt purge -y` + `sudo apt autoremove -y`\n281: - Repo-based apps (apt repo added at install) → also remove the `.list` file and keyring in uninstall\n282: - Official scripts → `curl ... | sh` inside `spawn`\n283: - Flatpak → `flatpak install -y flathub <app-id>` inside `spawn`, remove with `flatpak uninstall -y <app-id>`\n284: - `.deb` files → download to temp, `sudo apt install -y ./file.deb` inside `spawn`\n285: - File/AppImage installs → remove the installed files, symlinks, and desktop entries in uninstall\n286: - `usermod` for groups → print re-login reminder\n287: \n288: ---\n289: \n290: ## Editing an Existing Tool\n291: \n292: 1. Find the script in `bin/`\n293: 2. Understand its contract (args, output, exit codes)\n294: 3. Make the change — keep it idempotent\n295: 4. Update `DOC/POS.md` (or the relevant doc) if behaviour changed\n296: 5. Run `shellcheck` on the modified file\n297: \n298: ---\n299: \n300: ## Best Practices\n301: \n302: ### Alerting\n303: \n304: To notify on events, source the shared helper instead of calling a platform tool directly:\n305: \n306: ```bash\n307: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/notify.sh\" 2>/dev/null || source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/notify.sh\"\n308: notify_send \"Backup completed\"\n309: notify_send \"**disk full**\" --markdown\n310: ```\n311: \n312: `notify_send` is deliberately dependency-free (defines only itself, so it never clobbers a tool's own `log`/`warn`/`err`) and **silent-fails**: if no platform is configured it warns and returns 0, never breaking the caller's flow or exit code. Source it opt-in in any tool that should alert; for failure alerts use `trap 'notify_send \"...\" ERR'`.\n313: \n314: **Multi-platform routing:** `notify_send` delivers to every platform listed in `NOTIFY_PLATFORM` (env or `~/.config/linux_post_install/notify.env`, default `telegram`, comma-separated to send to all). Adding a new platform (e.g. Matrix/Synapse) means creating a `bin/pos-communication-<platform>` tool that implements the **sender contract**:\n315: \n316: ```bash\n317: pos-communication-<platform> send <value> [--markdown]   # exit 0 on delivery\n318: ```\n319: \n320: then listing it in `NOTIFY_PLATFORM`. Platform keys map to tool names via `notify_sender_name()` in `lib/notify.sh` — the telegram platform key stays `telegram` but its tool is `pos-communication-telegram-sender`. `pos-communication-telegram-sender` already follows this (`--markdown` is an alias for `--parse-mode markdown`). No changes to `lib/notify.sh` are needed for a new platform.\n321: \n322: ### Idempotency\n323: \n324: Check before creating, use `>>` with grep guards, don't overwrite user configs.\n325: \n326: ### Managed Config Blocks\n327: \n328: To let a tool own a slice of a user/system config file (e.g. Samba shares in `/etc/samba/smb.conf`) without clobbering hand edits, delimit the tool's section with start/end marker lines and rewrite only that slice:\n329: \n330: ```\n331: # >>> pos-managed share: <name>\n332: [media]\n333:    path = /mnt/hdd\n334: # <<< end pos-managed share\n335: ```\n336: \n337: - Idempotent upsert: one `awk` pass drops the existing block (or nothing if absent), then append the new block; removal uses the same `awk` with only the slice dropped.\n338: - The block-deletion guard matters: `$0 == s {inblock=1}` … `$0 == e && inblock == 1 {inblock=0; next}` — without the `inblock == 1` check, deleting one block also eats the end markers of other blocks further down the file.\n339: - Validate before writing: run the config's own checker on a temp copy (`testparm -s` for Samba), then apply with `sudo cp`; hot-reload instead of restarting (`smbcontrol smbd reload-config`).\n340: - Precedent: `bin/pos-share-smb-server`.\n341: \n342: ### Error Handling\n343: \n344: ```bash\n345: set -euo pipefail\n346: command -v docker &>/dev/null || { echo \"docker not found\"; exit 1; }\n347: [[ -n \"${1:-}\" ]] || { echo \"Usage: my-tool <arg>\"; exit 1; }\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ### Portability\n351: \n352: Targets **Debian** and **Ubuntu**. Use `apt`, assume bash at `/usr/bin/env bash`, check tools with `command -v`.\n353: \n354: ### Dry-run Support\n355: \n356: Scripts support `--dry-run`. Use the `run()` helper:\n357: \n358: ```bash\n359: run() {\n360:     if [ \"$DRY_RUN\" -eq 1 ]; then\n361:         log \"(dry-run) $*\"\n362:     else\n363:         \"$@\"\n364:     fi\n365: }\n366: run sudo apt install -y git\n367: ```\n368: \n369: ### Security\n370: \n371: - Never hardcode secrets — put them in `config/` (gitignored) or, for runtime tool config, `~/.config/linux_post_install/<tool>.env`\n372: - `chmod 600` for sensitive files\n373: - Mask secrets in `config` output (see `pos-communication-telegram-sender`'s `mask_token`)\n374: - Validate input before shell commands\n375: - Use `sudo` only where needed\n376: \n377: ### Naming\n378: \n379: - CLI tools: `bin/pos-<category>-<command>`\n380: - Legacy wrappers: `bin/wr-*`\n381: - App installers: `apps/<category>/<name>.sh`\n382: - Features: `features/<name>.sh`\n383: - Lowercase with hyphens\n384: \n385: ---\n386: \n387: ## Features & Flags\n388: \n389: `features/` holds scripts the user is likely to customize (e.g. `autostart.sh`). Unlike `bin/` (synced on every install), features are installed on demand and **never overwritten without asking**.\n390: \n391: ### Adding a Feature\n392: \n393: Start from the template: `cp templates/feature.sh features/<name>.sh`.\n394: \n395: 1. Create `features/<name>.sh` following the CLI tool template (shebang, `set -euo pipefail`, `--help`).\n396: 2. Nothing else is registered — `./install.sh --feature` auto-discovers it, copies it to `/usr/local/bin/`, asks before overwriting an existing file, and sets its flag.\n397: 3. If the feature backs a systemd service, gate the service on the flag in `postinstall.sh` (see below).\n398: \n399: ### Flag System\n400: \n401: System-wide flag store at `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/flags/` (presence = set, content = optional value). Sourced via `lib/flags.sh` (or the installed `/usr/local/bin/flags.sh`):\n402: \n403: ```bash\n404: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/lib/flags.sh\" 2>/dev/null || source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/flags.sh\"\n405: \n406: flag_set autostart        # green flag\n407: flag_set app \"2.1\"        # green flag with a value\n408: flag_is_set autostart     # test (0/1) — the primitive consumers use\n409: flag_value app            # → \"2.1\"\n410: flag_list                 # names of all set flags\n411: flag_clear autostart\n412: ```\n413: \n414: Writes use `run` + `sudo`, so they respect `--dry-run`. CLI equivalents: `flag-reader`, `flag-set`, `flag-clear`.\n415: \n416: **Example — service gated on a flag** (in `postinstall.sh`'s systemd loop):\n417: \n418: ```bash\n419: if [ \"$svc_name\" = \"myapp.service\" ] && ! flag_is_set myapp; then\n420:     warn \"myapp feature not installed — skipping myapp.service\"\n421:     continue\n422: fi\n423: ```\n424: \n425: ---\n426: \n427: ## Working with Systemd\n428: \n429: Create `systemd/<name>.service` — `postinstall.sh` copies it to `/etc/systemd/system/` and enables it automatically.\n430: \n431: ```ini\n432: [Unit]\n433: Description=My Service\n434: After=network-online.target\n435: Wants=network-online.target\n436: \n437: [Service]\n438: Type=simple\n439: ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/your-script.sh\n440: Restart=on-failure\n441: RestartSec=10\n442: \n443: [Install]\n444: WantedBy=multi-user.target\n445: ```\n446: \n447: ---\n448: \n449: ## Working with Config Files\n450: \n451: 1. Place the file in `config/`\n452: 2. Add copy logic to `postinstall.sh`:\n453: \n454: ```bash\n455: if [ -f config/your-config.conf ]; then\n456:     mkdir -p \"$HOME/.config/your-app\"\n457:     cp config/your-config.conf \"$HOME/.config/your-app/your-config.conf\"\n458:     chmod 600 \"$HOME/.config/your-app/your-config.conf\"\n459: fi\n460: ```\n461: \n462: ---\n463: \n464: ## Docker Compose / ScaleTail\n465: \n466: The installer clones [ScaleTail](https://github.com/tailscale-dev/ScaleTail) templates to `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail/` — 119+ self-hosted services with a Tailscale sidecar pattern. Each service gets a `tail-xxxxx.ts.net` URL via `network_mode: service:tailscale`.\n467: \n468: ### Config Strategy — Three Layers\n469: \n470: Values cascade from least to most specific:\n471: \n472: ```\n473: Template .env          (per-service defaults from ScaleTail)\n474:        ↓\n475: Global config          (~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env)\n476:        ↓\n477: Per-service .env       (/srv/<service>/.env) — created on first deploy, NEVER overwritten\n478: ```\n479: \n480: On first `pos docker compose up <service>`:\n481: 1. Template `.env` is copied to `/srv/<service>/.env`\n482: 2. Matching keys from global config are filled in\n483: 3. If `TS_AUTHKEY` is still empty, you're prompted to enter it\n484: 4. After that, the per-service `.env` is **never touched** — not even by `update`\n485: \n486: ### Layout\n487: \n488: | Path | Purpose | Mutability |\n489: |------|---------|------------|\n490: | `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail/services/<name>/` | ScaleTail templates (git repo) | Read-only |\n491: | `~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env` | Your global defaults | Edit via `config set` or `config edit` |\n492: | `/srv/<service>/` | Active deployment | Per-service `.env` preserved forever |\n493: \n494: ### Key Commands\n495: \n496: | Command | Behaviour |\n497: |---------|-----------|\n498: | `pos docker compose up <service>` | Deploys to `$SERVICES_BASE/<service>/`, creates `config/` + `data/`, generates `.env` from global defaults |\n499: | `pos docker compose down <service>` | Stops the stack |\n500: | `pos docker compose update` | `git pull` templates + refreshes `compose.yaml` for all deployed services (`.env` untouched) |\n501: | `pos docker compose config set K=V` | Sets a global default in `~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env` |\n502: | `pos docker compose config show` | Displays current global config and `SERVICES_BASE` |\n503: | `pos docker compose config edit` | Opens global config in `$EDITOR` |\n504: \n505: ### Global Config Keys\n506: \n507: | Key | Required | Default | Purpose |\n508: |-----|----------|---------|---------|\n509: | `TS_AUTHKEY` | Yes | — | Tailscale auth key for sidecar networking |\n510: | `TZ` | No | `Europe/Amsterdam` | Timezone for services |\n511: | `DNS_SERVER` | No | `9.9.9.9` | Custom DNS server |\n512: | `SERVICES_BASE` | No | `/srv` | Root directory for all deployments |\n513: \n514: ---\n515: \n516: ## Commit Guidelines\n517: \n518: - Use conventional prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`\n519: - Explain *why*, not just *what*\n520: - One logical change per commit\n521: \n522: ```\n523: feat: add pos-disk-usage for monitoring disk space\n524: fix: pos-network-ip fails when no default route exists\n525: docs: add example output for pos-network-scan\n526: ```\n527: \n528: ---\n529: \n530: ## Useful Commands\n531: \n532: ```bash\n533: # Syntax check a single script\n534: bash -n bin/my-script\n535: \n536: # ShellCheck linting\n537: shellcheck bin/my-script\n538: \n539: # Check all scripts\n540: for f in bin/* apps/*/*.sh lib/common.sh install.sh preinstall.sh postinstall.sh; do\n541:     bash -n \"$f\" || echo \"FAIL: $f\"\n542: done\n543: \n544: # Init submodule\n545: git submodule update --init\n546: \n547: # Pull latest ScaleTail templates\n548: git submodule update --remote compose/scale-tail\n549: \n550: # Test install in Docker\n551: docker run --rm -it -v $PWD:/repo ubuntu:22.04 bash\n552: # inside: cd /repo && ./install.sh\n553: \n554: # Test app installers\n555: ./apps/install.sh\n556: ./apps/install.sh --all\n557: ./apps/install.sh docker vscode\n558: ```\n\n(End of file - total 558 lines)\n</content>",
