          "type": "text",
          "text": "that was your plan: Plan: usb-automount feature (integrated like autostart)\nGoal\nA features/usb-automount.sh feature + flag-gated systemd/usb-automount.service, wired exactly like the autostart pattern. When a USB stick is plugged in (or at boot for sticks already in), it auto-mounts at /media/<label> world-writable (-o umask=000), so pos system backup's post-backup USB detection finds it and the user's copy succeeds. No pos tool — features stay bare scripts.\nNew files\n1. features/usb-automount.sh (~90 lines, set -euo pipefail, per templates/feature.sh)\n- Root guard: [ \"$(id -u)\" -eq 0 ] || exec sudo \"$0\" \"$@\" (service runs as root; manual run self-elevates). Test-only env seam AUTOMOUNT_SKIP_ROOT_GUARD=1 to bypass.\n- udev self-install (first root run, idempotent): write /etc/udev/rules.d/99-usb-automount.rules:\nACTION==\"add\", KERNEL==\"sd[a-z]*\", SUBSYSTEM==\"block\", ENV{ID_BUS}==\"usb\", TAG+=\"systemd\", SYSTEMD_WANTS=\"usb-automount.service\"\nthen udevadm control --reload + udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=block (seams UDEV_RULES_DIR, UDEVADM). Rewrite only if content differs.\n- Mount scan (lsblk -J -o NAME,PATH,LABEL,MOUNTPOINT,RM,TYPE + recursive jq filter — same JSON technique as the backup tool): select rm==true && type==\"part\" (and whole-disk type==\"disk\" with no children) where mountpoint==null; skip already-mounted; skip /boot/efi-style fixed media (non-USB filtered by the udev rule anyway, but the scan re-checks ID_BUS isn't available via lsblk — use a second lsblk -J pass? No — the boot run should mount USB only; use udevadm info? Simplest: try mount, and for the scan accept all removable; the udev rule already restricts to USB for hotplug, boot run uses all removable — note in docs that the script mounts removable storage). Actually: filter to rm==true only; that's removable by definition. Good enough and documentable.\n- Mount: mkdir -p /media/<label> (dedupe with -2, -3 on collision); try mount -o umask=000 