            "output": "<path>/mnt/hdd/ciya_space/work/repo/Linux_post_install/DOC/SYSTEMD.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Systemd & Shell Integration Reference\n2: \n3: The units installed and enabled by `postinstall.sh`, plus the `pos` bash completion.\n4: \n5: - [Services](#services)\n6:   - [`autostart.service`](#autostartservice)\n7:   - [`ssh-agent.service`](#ssh-agentservice)\n8:   - [`pos-health.service`](#pos-healthservice)\n9: - [Per-user units (`pos network download`)](#per-user-units-pos-network-download)\n10: - [Feature-flag gating](#feature-flag-gating)\n11: - [Bash completion](#bash-completion)\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## Services\n16: \n17: `postinstall.sh` copies every `systemd/*.service` (and `systemd/*.timer`) to `/etc/systemd/system/`, runs `systemctl daemon-reload`, then enables each one (see the gating rule below).\n18: \n19: ### autostart.service\n20: \n21: **Purpose:** run `features/autostart.sh` at boot (after the network is online) and keep retrying if it fails.\n22: \n23: ```ini\n24: [Unit]\n25: Description=My Linux Autostart Script\n26: After=network-online.target\n27: Wants=network-online.target\n28: \n29: [Service]\n30: Type=simple\n31: ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/autostart.sh\n32: Restart=on-failure\n33: RestartSec=10\n34: \n35: [Install]\n36: WantedBy=multi-user.target\n37: ```\n38: \n39: **Configuration:** point `ExecStart` at your boot script. Because the target script is a *feature*, this unit is only **enabled** when the `autostart` flag is set — the file is still copied, but a skipped feature leaves the unit present-but-disabled.\n40: \n41: ### ssh-agent.service\n42: \n43: **Purpose:** a system-wide SSH agent, one shared socket for all sessions (so `pos ssh load-keys` and everyday ssh work without per-login agents).\n44: \n45: ```ini\n46: [Unit]\n47: Description=SSH Authentication Agent\n48: After=network.target\n49: \n50: [Service]\n51: Type=simple\n52: ExecStartPre=mkdir -p /run/ssh-agent\n53: ExecStart=/usr/bin/ssh-agent -D -a /run/ssh-agent/socket\n54: ExecStartPost=/bin/sh -c 'chmod 666 /run/ssh-agent/socket'\n55: ExecStopPost=/bin/sh -c 'rm -f /run/ssh-agent/socket'\n56: Restart=on-failure\n57: \n58: [Install]\n59: WantedBy=multi-user.target\n60: ```\n61: \n62: **Configuration:** socket at `/run/ssh-agent/socket` (world-readable/writable). `~/.bashrc` (set by `postinstall.sh`) exports `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` to it. Not gated on any feature flag.\n63: \n64: ### pos-health.service\n65: \n66: **Purpose:** daily \"health digest\" — runs `pos system health --send --markdown` at 08:00 and sends the report to the configured notify platform(s).\n67: \n68: ```ini\n69: [Unit]\n70: Description=POS Health digest (daily report via configured notify platforms)\n71: After=network-online.target\n72: Wants=network-online.target\n73: \n74: [Service]\n75: Type=oneshot\n76: User=__POS_USER__\n77: EnvironmentFile=-%h/.config/linux_post_install/system.env\n78: EnvironmentFile=-%h/.config/linux_post_install/notify.env\n79: ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/pos system health --send --markdown\n80: \n81: [Timer]\n82: OnCalendar=*-*-* 08:00:00\n83: Persistent=true\n84: ```\n85: \n86: The service is `Type=oneshot` and is driven **only** by its companion `pos-health.timer` (`WantedBy=timers.target`); the service itself is never enabled directly.\n87: \n88: **Configuration:** `postinstall.sh` substitutes `__POS_USER__` with the installing user (`${SUDO_USER:-$USER}`) so the digest uses that user's real notify config. The `EnvironmentFile=` lines load `system.env` (health/backup settings) and `notify.env` (`NOTIFY_PLATFORM`). The timer is enabled only when a Telegram config (`~/.config/linux_post_install/telegram.env`) already exists — otherwise postinstall warns and skips; re-run postinstall after configuring a notify platform to install it.\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: ## Per-user units (`pos network download`)\n93: \n94: The aria2 download tool installs **user-scope** units (not by postinstall):\n95: written to `~/.config/systemd/user/` and enabled with `systemctl --user` on\n96: first use, so they need no sudo.\n97: \n98: - `pos-aria2.service` — the daemon (`Type=simple`, `Restart=on-failure`,\n99:   `WantedBy=default.target`): runs `aria2c --enable-rpc\n100:   --rpc-listen-port=6800 --rpc-secret=… --dir=$HOME/Downloads --continue=true\n101:   --max-connection-per-server=16 --split=16 --seed-time=0`. `pos network\n102:   download start` generates the `RPC_SECRET` into\n103:   `~/.config/linux_post_install/download.env` (chmod 600).\n104: - `pos-aria2-retry.service` — `Type=oneshot`; `ExecStart=<pos> network download\n105:   retry all --once --quiet` (runner = `/usr/local/bin/pos-network-download`,\n106:   repo-path fallback with a warning). Driven only by its companion timer.\n107: - `pos-aria2-retry.timer` — `OnUnitActiveSec=2min` + `OnBootSec=2min`,\n108:   `AccuracySec=30s`, `Persistent=true`, `WantedBy=timers.target`. Arms itself\n109:   when a download starts (`add`/`torrent`/`metalink`/`restart`) and disables\n110:   itself when no active, waiting, or errored downloads remain.\n111: \n112: On headless boxes `pos network download start` prints a `loginctl\n113: enable-linger` warning so the user units survive logout.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## Feature-flag gating\n118: \n119: The systemd loop in `postinstall.sh` special-cases two units:\n120: \n121: ```bash\n122: if [ \"$svc_name\" = \"autostart.service\" ] && ! flag_is_set autostart; then\n123:     warn \"autostart feature not installed — skipping autostart.service (run ./install.sh --feature)\"\n124:     continue\n125: fi\n126: if [ \"$svc_name\" = \"pos-health.service\" ]; then\n127:     # substitute User= and enable pos-health.timer only if Telegram is configured\n128: fi\n129: ```\n130: \n131: - `autostart.service` is **enabled** only when the `autostart` feature flag is set (`./install.sh --feature` or `flag-set autostart`). See [SCRIPTS.md → lib/flags.sh](SCRIPTS.md#libflagssh--feature-flags).\n132: - `pos-health.service` is **not** enabled at all — `postinstall.sh` enables `pos-health.timer` instead, and only when a Telegram config already exists.\n133: \n134: ---\n135: \n136: ## Bash completion\n137: \n138: **File:** `completions/pos.bash`\n139: **Purpose:** tab-completion for the `pos` CLI.\n140: \n141: ### How it works\n142: \n143: - **Dynamically discovers** subcommands by listing executable `pos-*` files next to the `pos` binary — no hard-coded command list, so new tools complete automatically.\n144: - Works with the `bash-completion` package (`_init_completion`) and falls back to a manual init if it isn't loaded.\n145: - Provides completion for the first two words of `pos <category> <command>`.\n146: \n147: ### Configuration\n148: \n149: Installed by `postinstall.sh` to `/usr/local/share/bash-completion/completions/pos.bash` and sourced from `~/.bashrc`. To load it manually: `source completions/pos.bash` (or copy into `/etc/bash_completion.d/`).\n\n(End of file - total 149 lines)\n</content>",
